-- allium: 3
-- feeds.allium
--
-- Scope: Programmable feed epics — generic mechanism for surfacing external
--        items (PRs, security alerts, anything emitting JSON) as tasks under
--        an Epic.
-- Includes: FeedItem schema, upsert semantics, status preservation, polling
--           cadence, error handling, the seeded example epic, the verify-feed
--           validator CLI, the role-routed reconcile path that splits a
--           single PR-review emission across My/Team/Bots sub-epics, and the
--           append-only mode for sources that emit events rather than
--           mirroring state (AppendOnlyFeed).
-- Excludes: Specific feed scripts — the shipped reference templates under
--           `scripts/`, what each queries, which config files it reads and
--           which Signals it emits, are specified in feed-scripts.allium.
--           The GENERIC feed runtime (flat upsert + group_by_repo) never
--           embeds upstream-specific knowledge — those scripts are
--           user-owned executables. The legacy Review Board and Security
--           Board UIs remain removed; their behaviour is subsumed by feed
--           epics.
--
-- DELIBERATE EXCEPTION — the role-routed path. The reviews_parent reconcile
-- (RoleRoutedFeedSync below, driven by the RouteSignals contract) is the ONE
-- place where PR-routing policy lives inside the runtime rather than in a
-- user script. This departs, on purpose, from the dumb-runtime principle so
-- the my/team/bot split is centralised, testable and leak-free. The exception
-- is contained: it fires ONLY for an epic whose core/Epic.feed_role =
-- reviews_parent; every other feed epic keeps the upstream-agnostic
-- flat/group_by_repo behaviour untouched. Rationale and the considered-and-
-- rejected minimal alternative are recorded in
-- docs/superpowers/specs/2026-06-15-pr-review-feed-routing-design.md.

use "./core.allium" as core

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Contracts
------------------------------------------------------------

-- RouteSignals is the pure routing function that maps a PR item's signals to
-- the FeedRole sub-epic it belongs in. It is PURE (no async, no DB, no I/O) and
-- TOTAL: every input, including the empty signal set, maps to exactly one of
-- my_reviews, team_reviews or bots. It NEVER returns none, reviews_parent or
-- cve — those roles exist for structural epic placement (see core/FeedRole),
-- not as routing outcomes.
--
-- Precedence is strict first-match-wins, in this order:
--
--   1. engaged = (reviewed or commented) and not author_me  -> my_reviews
--      Engagement wins over the bot rule: a bot-authored PR I have reviewed
--      still lands in my reviews. Note "engagement" excludes my OWN activity —
--      a comment I made on my own PR (author_me) is not engagement, so this
--      branch does not fire for it.
--   2. author_bot                                           -> bots
--   3. direct_request or org_review                          -> my_reviews
--      org_review (an org-scoped review-requested/reviewed-by/commenter
--      match, outside the repos.conf repo list) is treated the same as a
--      direct review request: it still loses to the bot rule above, so an
--      org-scoped bot PR routes to bots unless it is also reviewed or
--      commented (engagement still wins — see rule 1).
--   4. team_request                                         -> team_reviews
--   5. fallback (no signal matches, including the empty set) -> my_reviews
--      Routing never fails; an unmatched item defaults to my_reviews. Logging
--      a warning for the empty/unmatched case is the caller's job (WP3), not
--      this function's.

contract RouteSignals {
    route: (signals: Set<Signal>) -> core/FeedRole

    @invariant Total
        -- route is defined for every possible signal set, including the empty
        -- set; there is no input for which it has no result.

    @invariant RangeRestricted
        -- route's result is always one of my_reviews, team_reviews or bots.
        -- It never yields none, reviews_parent or cve.

    @invariant Pure
        -- route depends only on its signals argument. No I/O, no DB access, no
        -- async, no observation of clock or external state. Same input always
        -- yields the same output.

    @invariant Precedence
        -- First match wins in the order: engaged (reviewed or commented, and
        -- not author_me) -> my_reviews; then author_bot -> bots; then
        -- direct_request or org_review -> my_reviews; then team_request ->
        -- team_reviews; then the empty/unmatched fallback -> my_reviews.
        -- Engagement is evaluated before the bot rule, so a reviewed bot PR
        -- routes to my_reviews rather than bots.

    @guidance
        -- Implementation: src/feed/routing.rs::route, re-exported as
        -- crate::feed::route. Coverage: src/feed/routing.rs tests
        -- (feed::routing::tests::*).
}

-- ExcludeOwnAuthored is the pure predicate that decides whether a PR item
-- reaches the review subtree AT ALL. It runs BEFORE RouteSignals: an excluded
-- item is never routed, never inserted, and never enters the reconcile's
-- keep-set, so an existing task for it is deleted by the same stale pass that
-- removes a merged PR.
--
-- Exactly one signal excludes: author_me. A PR the user wrote is not review
-- work for the user. Such PRs did reach my_reviews in practice — the
-- involvement qualifiers are not all self-exclusive (only
-- `commenter:@me -author:@me` spells the exclusion out), and route's fallback
-- sends an otherwise-unmatched item to my_reviews. Which qualifier let a given
-- own-authored PR in does not matter here: the item arrives carrying author_me
-- either way, and that is what this contract acts on.
--
-- The exclusion is UNCONDITIONAL — it is not "drop from my_reviews only". An
-- own-authored PR that also carries team_request or author_bot is dropped too,
-- because the user authoring it is what makes it not review work, regardless
-- of who else was asked.
--
-- LIMIT OF THE MECHANISM. The exclusion is only as good as the signal, and
-- author_me is derived by the SCRIPT by comparing each PR author login against
-- the authenticated user's. That lookup soft-fails to empty rather than
-- failing the feed, so a transient failure emits no author_me on any item that
-- cycle and own-authored PRs surface until the next successful poll. This is
-- accepted: degrading author_me detection is strictly better than dropping the
-- whole emission, and the exclusion is self-healing on the following cycle.
--
-- WHY IN THE RUNTIME AND NOT IN THE SCRIPT. Adding `-author:@me` to the
-- review qualifiers in scripts/fetch-reviews.sh would exclude the same PRs,
-- and is deliberately NOT the chosen mechanism. Feed scripts are USER-OWNED:
-- the shipped script is a template the user copies to scripts/local/ and
-- edits, so a fix to the template does not reach the copy that actually runs.
-- The runtime is the one place the exclusion holds for every existing
-- deployment and for any other script that emits author_me. This is the same
-- reasoning as the DELIBERATE EXCEPTION in Scope: PR-routing policy for the
-- reviews_parent path lives in the runtime so it is centralised and
-- leak-free. The author_me signal already existed for exactly this
-- distinction; this contract is what finally acts on it.
--
-- Consequence for RouteSignals: rule 1's `and not author_me` guard is now
-- UNREACHABLE from the role-routed path — no author_me item survives to be
-- routed. The guard is retained rather than removed: route is a total pure
-- function over the whole Signal set (its Total invariant), so it must stay
-- defined and correct for an input this caller happens never to hand it.

contract ExcludeOwnAuthored {
    excluded: (signals: Set<Signal>) -> Boolean

    @invariant Total
        -- excluded is defined for every possible signal set, including the
        -- empty set (which is not excluded).

    @invariant Pure
        -- excluded depends only on its signals argument. No I/O, no DB
        -- access, no async, no observation of clock or external state.

    @invariant OnlyAuthorMe
        -- excluded(signals) is true exactly when author_me is in signals.
        -- No other signal excludes, and no combination of other signals
        -- rescues an author_me item.

    @invariant PrecedesRouting
        -- excluded is evaluated before route. An excluded item is never
        -- passed to route, so no routing outcome can contradict it.

    @guidance
        -- Implementation: src/feed/routing.rs::excluded_from_reviews,
        -- re-exported as crate::feed::excluded_from_reviews, applied in
        -- src/feed/ingest/role_routed.rs::run_role_routed_feed_sync before
        -- src/feed/ingest/routing.rs::route_and_group_entries. Coverage:
        -- src/feed/routing.rs tests (feed::routing::tests::*) for the
        -- predicate, src/feed/ingest/tests.rs for the reconcile behaviour.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Enumerations
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Signal is a routing hint a feed script attaches to a FeedItem. Signals are
-- routed into the right feed bucket (the sub-epic whose core/FeedRole matches)
-- by the RouteSignals contract above. They carry no meaning for ingest itself
-- beyond being stored on the Task and fed to that routing function.
--
-- author_me is the one signal that is not merely a routing hint: it EXCLUDES
-- the item from the review subtree entirely, before routing runs at all (see
-- the ExcludeOwnAuthored contract above).
enum Signal { direct_request | team_request | reviewed | commented | author_bot | author_me | org_review }

------------------------------------------------------------
-- FeedItem
------------------------------------------------------------

-- A FeedItem is the wire format that a feed_command emits. The runner
-- spawns the command, parses stdout as a JSON array of FeedItem, and
-- upserts each item as a Task under the owning Epic.
--
-- The command is responsible for whatever upstream fetching is needed
-- (e.g. calling `gh api`, scraping a dashboard). The dispatch runtime is
-- only responsible for invocation, parsing, and persistence.

value FeedItem {
    external_id: String       -- stable, unique-per-feed identifier (e.g. "pr:acme/app#42")
    title: String
    description: String
    url: String = ""          -- optional external URL; empty string when absent.
                              -- When the URL points to a known GitHub repo whose
                              -- local clone is registered in the dispatch repo
                              -- list, the runner uses it to resolve a repo_path
                              -- for the inserted Task. See UpsertFeedTasks.
    url_type: core/UrlType?   -- optional explicit type for `url`. When set, the
                              -- inserted Task's url_type is taken verbatim from
                              -- this field; when absent, the type is inferred
                              -- from the URL string (see UpsertFeedTasks).
                              -- Lets a feed declare types inference cannot
                              -- reach — e.g. the security feed declaring
                              -- security_alert for Dependabot alert URLs that
                              -- would otherwise classify as other. Defaults to
                              -- absent when the script omits the field,
                              -- preserving wire compatibility with scripts
                              -- written before this field existed. Ignored
                              -- when url is empty (there is no URL to type).
    status: core/TaskStatus   -- desired column for newly-inserted tasks
    tag: core/TaskTag         -- required: drives DispatchMode for the inserted
                              -- Task. Feed scripts must set this so dispatch
                              -- routes the task to the correct agent (e.g.
                              -- pr_review for Dependabot PRs, fix for
                              -- security alerts). Missing or unknown tag
                              -- causes parse failure in verify-feed and the
                              -- FeedRunner.
                              --
                              -- A REVIEW tag (core/TaskTag.is_review — today
                              -- dependabot and pr_review) additionally
                              -- REQUIRES `url` to name a pull request: see
                              -- AReviewTaggedFeedItemNamesItsPr in the
                              -- FeedItemParse block below. Nothing else
                              -- constrains the pair.
    labels: List<String> = [] -- optional free-form labels copied to Task.labels
                              -- on insert and re-applied on every conflict update.
                              -- Ordered: the JSON array order is preserved through
                              -- to badge rendering order on the card
                              -- (feed-controlled, not user-editable). Defaults
                              -- to the empty list when the script omits the field,
                              -- preserving wire compatibility with scripts
                              -- written before this field existed.
    sort_order: Integer?      -- optional ordering hint copied to Task.sort_order;
                              -- the existing column ordering uses
                              -- COALESCE(sort_order, id) ASC so lower values sort
                              -- first. Used by the CVE feed to surface CRITICAL
                              -- alerts above HIGH/MEDIUM/LOW (1=CRITICAL, 2=HIGH,
                              -- 3=MEDIUM, 4=LOW). Optional; omission leaves
                              -- Task.sort_order unchanged.
    wrap_up_mode: core/WrapUpMode? -- optional; sets the inserted Task's
                              -- wrap_up_mode. Applied on INSERT only: on a
                              -- conflict (re-poll of the same external_id) the
                              -- existing Task's wrap_up_mode is preserved, like
                              -- status/sub_status/repo_path, so a user's manual
                              -- change survives feed refreshes — as long as the
                              -- item remains present in every emission (an item
                              -- that drops out and later reappears is deleted
                              -- and reinserted, taking the feed value again;
                              -- same tradeoff as status/repo_path). Absent
                              -- leaves the Task's wrap_up_mode null (decide at
                              -- wrap-up time). Used by the CVE-alert feed
                              -- scripts (fetch-cve.sh, fetch-security.sh) to
                              -- default fix tasks to pr so wrap-up goes
                              -- straight to a PR — a CVE-shaped item should
                              -- always default to pr regardless of which of
                              -- the two emits it, and by fetch-log-warnings.sh
                              -- to default its triage cards to rebase. Why each
                              -- script picks the path it does is in
                              -- feed-scripts.allium, not here.
                              -- Defaults to absent when the
                              -- script omits the field, preserving wire
                              -- compatibility with scripts written before
                              -- this field existed.
    signals: Set<Signal> = {} -- optional routing signals a feed script attaches
                              -- to this item; routed into the right feed bucket
                              -- by the RouteSignals contract below. Defaults to the
                              -- empty set when the script omits the field,
                              -- preserving wire compatibility with scripts
                              -- written before this field existed.
                              --
                              -- UNRECOGNISED signal values are DROPPED with a
                              -- warning rather than failing the whole item. This
                              -- is a deliberate, scoped exception to the
                              -- otherwise strict feed-JSON parse-failure rule
                              -- (and to the Storage Boundary Validation rule in
                              -- core.allium): a newer feed script's signal value
                              -- must not break ingest on an older dispatch
                              -- binary. This contrasts with `tag`, where an
                              -- unknown value DOES cause parse failure, because
                              -- tag drives dispatch routing and a silent drop
                              -- there would mis-route the task.
                              --
                              -- BOTH halves of that contrast -- lenient signals
                              -- and strict tag -- are properties of the FeedItem
                              -- decode itself, not of any one caller, so they
                              -- apply UNIFORMLY to all three feed entry points
                              -- (auto-poll, manual "r", verify-feed). No caller
                              -- can opt out of either, and none can drift from
                              -- the others on this. Where the drop WARNING lands
                              -- does differ by entry point: the two runtime paths
                              -- log it to app.log, while verify-feed surfaces it
                              -- on stderr (see the VerifyFeed rule above).
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Producing FeedItems
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Feed scripts are user-owned executables that write a JSON array of
-- FeedItem objects to stdout, exit 0 on success, and emit any errors on
-- stderr with non-zero exit. The dispatch binary never knows about the
-- specific upstream — every feed source (PRs, security alerts, calendar
-- events, anything else) is a script the user authors or installs.
--
-- The dispatch repo ships reference templates under `scripts/`. What each one
-- queries, which config files it reads and which Signals it emits are
-- specified in feed-scripts.allium — they are user-owned scripts, not part of
-- this runtime.

-- == verify-feed CLI ==

rule VerifyFeed {
    when: VerifyFeedCommand(command)

    -- The verify-feed subcommand spawns <command> via `sh -c`, captures
    -- stdout, and validates that it parses as Vec<FeedItem>. On success
    -- it prints a table of (external_id, title, tag, status) and exits 0.
    -- On failure (non-zero exit, parse failure including missing or
    -- unknown tag) it prints the error to stderr and exits 1.
    --
    -- An emission that PARSES but contains ZERO items is ALSO a failure
    -- (exit 1), on the assumption that an empty feed indicates a
    -- misconfigured command (e.g. fetch-cve.sh with no repos configured)
    -- rather than a genuine absence of work. This is the third failure
    -- mode alongside the two above.
    --
    -- An item carrying an UNRECOGNISED `signals` value is NOT a failure:
    -- the signal is dropped per the FeedItem.signals contract, the item
    -- still counts toward the valid-item total, and the exit status stays
    -- 0. The drop IS reported, on stderr. verify-feed is the one feed
    -- entry point with no app.log sink, so without this the lenient-decode
    -- warning would be written to a no-op tracing dispatcher and lost --
    -- a user debugging a typo'd signal would see only "N valid items" and
    -- no indication anything was discarded. Reporting it on stderr rather
    -- than stdout keeps the parsed-item table pipeable.
    --
    -- This is the supported way to debug a feed script before wiring it
    -- onto an epic's feed_command. It performs no DB writes.

    ensures: FeedVerified(command: command)

    @guidance
        -- Implementation: src/main.rs Commands::VerifyFeed match arm.
        -- Coverage: tests/cli.rs verify_feed_*.
        -- Spawns <command> via sh -c, parses stdout as FeedItem array via
        -- the shared src/feed/parse.rs::parse_feed_items (see the
        -- FeedItemParse block below) -- not its own serde_json call.
        -- On success: print_table(items), exit 0.
        -- On failure (non-zero exit or parse error): print_stderr, exit 1.
        -- cmd_verify_feed installs a stderr tracing subscriber before
        -- parsing (it is the only feed path that does not call
        -- init_app_log_subscriber), so lenient-decode warnings such as the
        -- dropped-signal warning surface on the terminal instead of being
        -- swallowed.
}

-- == Setup seeding ==

rule SeedExampleFeedEpic {
    when: SetupCommand(data_dir)

    -- On first-run setup, dispatch creates exactly one example feed epic
    -- so the kanban board is not empty and the user has a working
    -- reference for the script + epic plumbing. The seeding is
    -- idempotent: if any epic already has feed_command pointing at the
    -- installed example script, no new epic is created.
    --
    -- THREE files are embedded in the dispatch binary at compile time and
    -- written to <data_dir>/scripts/ on setup: the example script
    -- (scripts/fetch-dependabot.sh) and the two config files it sources
    -- from its own directory, repos.conf (which repos to poll) and
    -- bots.conf (which bot authors to filter on). The script is inert
    -- without both, so installing the script alone would seed an epic
    -- wired to a command the user has no file to configure. bots.conf is
    -- the same file fetch-reviews.sh's bot-author pass reads.
    --
    -- The installer never overwrites a file that already exists at the
    -- target path, so user edits are preserved across re-runs of
    -- `dispatch tui`'s startup configuration check — for the conf files as
        -- much as the script (see startup.allium).
    -- (`<data_dir>` is the parent directory of the dispatch sqlite DB.)

    ensures:
        let script_path = install_example_script(data_dir)
        if not exists Epic where Epic.feed_command = script_path:
            Epic.created(
                title:              "Dependabot",
                feed_command:       script_path,
                feed_interval_secs: 300,
                sort_order:         0
            )

    @guidance
        -- Implementation: src/setup/plugins.rs::seed_feed_epics calls
        -- src/setup/plugins.rs::install_example_script(data_dir)
        -- (both re-exported via `pub use` in src/setup/mod.rs).
        -- The embedded script source: scripts/fetch-dependabot.sh
        -- (included via include_str!).
        -- Coverage: src/setup/plugins.rs tests prefixed with seed_feed_epics_
        -- and install_example_script_.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules
------------------------------------------------------------

-- == Polling ==

rule FeedTick {
    when: TickInterval()

    -- The FeedRunner walks every Epic with feed_command != null. For each
    -- such epic, if the elapsed time since its last successful or attempted
    -- run is at least the resolved interval, the command is spawned.
    --
    -- Resolved interval =
    --   epic.feed_interval_secs (when set) else config.default_feed_interval
    --
    -- An epic whose STORED interval is below core/config.min_feed_interval is
    -- not polled at all. It is skipped and a warning naming the epic is
    -- emitted, on EVERY tick it remains misconfigured. Write-time validation
    -- (epics.allium: CreateEpic, UpdateEpicViaMcp, SetManagedFeedConfig) is
    -- what normally keeps such a row from existing, so reaching this branch
    -- means the value arrived by a path that bypassed the service -- a
    -- hand-edited database, or a bug -- and the runner must not make it
    -- work anyway. Skipping rather than clamping is deliberate: a clamped
    -- feed would run at a cadence nobody chose while looking healthy.
    --
    -- The floor also covers the negative case, which failed differently and
    -- worse: a negative interval read as an effectively infinite one, so the
    -- feed went permanently silent with nothing logged. Both now take the
    -- same visible path.

    ensures:
        for epic in Epic where epic.is_feed:
            if epic.feed_interval_secs != null
               and seconds(epic.feed_interval_secs) < core/config.min_feed_interval:
                FeedIntervalBelowMinimumWarned(epic: epic)
            else:
                let interval =
                    if epic.feed_interval_secs != null:
                        seconds(epic.feed_interval_secs)
                    else:
                        core/config.default_feed_interval
                if elapsed_since_last_run(epic) >= interval:
                    FeedCycleRequested(epic: epic)

    @guidance
        -- Each epic's last_run timestamp is bumped before the spawn so
        -- that a misconfigured command is not retried on every tick. Note the
        -- interaction with SerialisedFeedCycle: last_run is bumped even when
        -- the request is then DROPPED because a cycle for that epic is already
        -- in flight, so a dropped tick waits a full interval rather than
        -- retrying on the next one. That is deliberate — a tick that retried
        -- immediately would spin against a slow in-flight cycle.
        -- Command execution MUST NOT block the event loop. Each eligible
        -- epic's command is spawned as a background tokio::task so that
        -- key events and MCP notifications remain responsive during fetch.
        -- FeedRunner runs in its own loop at FEED_POLL_INTERVAL (2s,
        -- src/feed/mod.rs::FEED_POLL_INTERVAL), independent from the TUI's
        -- TickInterval / TICK_INTERVAL. Both are 2s today, but the constants
        -- are kept separate so the two concerns can evolve independently.
        -- Implementation: src/feed/mod.rs::FeedRunner (struct and
        -- src/feed/mod.rs::FeedRunner::tick).
        --
        -- Performance cache + invalidation invariant. The runner caches "does
        -- any epic have a feed_command?" (any_feed_cmds); when it is
        -- Some(false) the tick short-circuits before any DB work. This cache
        -- MUST be invalidated on every surface that can enable the first feed
        -- on a previously feed-less instance, or that feed is stranded
        -- (provisioned but never polled) until an unrelated event or a restart:
        -- MCP is the only way to enable a feed: there is no TUI config surface.
        -- The invariant is enforced on the EVENT, not per calling handler: EVERY
        -- McpEvent::Refresh and EVERY McpEvent::EpicChanged invalidates the
        -- cache, whichever handler emitted it. Both arms route through
        -- TuiRuntime::invalidate_feed_cache, which fires the watch sender
        -- FeedRunner.tick checks at the top of each tick to reset any_feed_cmds
        -- to None (re-query).
        --
        -- The obligation on a handler is therefore to EMIT one of those two
        -- events — a handler that emits neither (or only TaskChanged) inherits
        -- nothing. Today's feed-enabling emitters:
        --   1. MCP set_managed_feed_config -> McpEvent::Refresh
        --   2. MCP epic mutations           -> McpEvent::EpicChanged
        -- Regression coverage, one test per link in that chain:
        -- src/mcp/handlers/tests/managed_feeds.rs::set_notifies_the_runtime_so_the_feed_cache_is_invalidated
        -- (the handler emits Refresh),
        -- src/runtime/tests/feeds.rs::mcp_refresh_invalidates_feed_runner_cache_after_enabling_a_feed
        -- (Refresh invalidates), and
        -- src/feed/mod.rs::tick_re_queries_after_epic_changed_invalidation.
}

-- == Serialisation ==

rule SerialisedFeedCycle {
    when: FeedCycleRequested(epic)

    -- A FEED CYCLE is the whole sequence a feed request performs for one epic:
    -- exec the feed command, parse its stdout, apply the two degraded-emission
    -- guards, dispatch FeedSync, and tear down the worktrees of any task the sync
    -- removed. Exactly TWO surfaces request one: FeedTick (per eligible epic,
    -- above) and ManualFeedRefresh (the "r" key). The verify-feed CLI execs a
    -- feed command but performs no sync and shares no process with the TUI, so
    -- it is outside this rule.
    --
    -- At most ONE cycle per epic runs at a time. A request arriving while a
    -- cycle for the SAME epic is in flight is DROPPED — not queued, not
    -- delayed: no exec, no parse, no sync, no teardown, no notification. The
    -- claim is symmetric, so whichever surface requested first wins; an
    -- auto-poll tick is dropped by an in-flight manual refresh exactly as the
    -- reverse. Requests for DIFFERENT epics never contend.
    --
    -- == Why the whole cycle, and why dropped ==
    --
    -- The steps of RoleRoutedFeedSync are separate non-transactional writes,
    -- each filtering on a task's CURRENT epic, and its stale-delete keep-set is
    -- computed from ONE emission. Two cycles interleaving there can have one
    -- pass see a task the other has moved as absent from its own (older)
    -- emission, delete it, and — since the delete feeds TaskTeardown — force
    -- remove a live review agent's worktree. Serialising ONLY the sync would
    -- still leave a cycle reconciling against an emission fetched before the
    -- other cycle's writes landed, so the claim spans the exec too. It also
    -- halves the upstream API spend of a double refresh.
    --
    -- Dropping rather than queueing: even with the exec bounded at
    -- feed_command_timeout (core.allium config), a queue behind a slow or
    -- hung command grows unboundedly with the number of dropped requests
    -- (N requests deep is N * feed_command_timeout deep). Dropping caps
    -- in-flight work per epic at one cycle regardless of how many requests
    -- arrive while it runs.
    --
    -- == Scope: this closes the feed-vs-feed race ONLY ==
    --
    -- The claim serialises feed cycles against EACH OTHER. It does nothing
    -- about a concurrent NON-feed writer. Any writer that moves a task into the
    -- reconciled subtree mid-cycle — an MCP task update carrying a new epic, an
    -- epic reparent, a flatten, a board move — can place a feed task under a
    -- role sub-epic while it is absent from the in-flight emission, and the
    -- stale delete will then remove it and tear down its worktree. Same
    -- mechanism, same consequence, one leg simply is not a feed cycle. The
    -- invariant below is therefore worded "another cycle", not "another
    -- writer". DegradedNonEmptyEmission does NOT help here: this race needs no
    -- degraded emission, so its additive gate never fires for it. The
    -- alternative that would have covered it — refusing to tear down a task
    -- carrying live agent state — was considered and rejected there, on the
    -- cost it charges to legitimate removals.

    ensures:
        if in_flight_cycle(epic):
            -- Dropped. Nothing runs, and nothing about the epic's tasks
            -- changes. The requesting surface presents this per its own rule
            -- (silently for FeedTick, as a status line for ManualFeedTrigger).
            FeedCycleDropped(epic: epic)
        else:
            -- Claimed for the whole cycle, and released when it ends however it
            -- ends: success, spawn failure, non-zero exit, parse failure,
            -- degraded emission, or a panic partway through. What the claimed
            -- cycle then DOES is specified elsewhere — FeedCommandFailure,
            -- DegradedEmptyEmission and FeedSync's dispatch — so this rule
            -- deliberately asserts only the serialisation decision.
            FeedCycleStarted(epic: epic)

    @guidance
        -- Implementation: the claim registry is
        -- src/feed/guard.rs::FeedSyncGuard, a set of claimed epic ids behind a
        -- short-lived mutex; src/feed/guard.rs::FeedClaim is the RAII token and
        -- releases on Drop, which is what makes "released however it ends" hold
        -- across the cycle's many early returns and a panicking task alike.
        -- The cycle itself is src/feed/cycle.rs::FeedCycle::run, which claims
        -- FIRST and then owns every step through teardown. Both request
        -- surfaces — the auto-poll tick in src/feed/mod.rs::FeedRunner::tick and
        -- src/runtime/epics.rs::exec_trigger_epic_feed (manual "r") — delegate
        -- to it and differ ONLY in how they present the outcome, the same
        -- single-shared-function discipline that FeedSync's dispatcher and
        -- FeedItemParse already follow. A third step added to a feed cycle must
        -- land in FeedCycle::run, never twice in the two callers.
        --
        -- The claim key is the POLLED epic's id — for a reviews_parent that is
        -- the parent, never a role sub-epic. Role sub-epics carry no
        -- feed_command (epics.allium ProvisionManagedEpics), and only the
        -- parent's cycle writes the subtree, so one key covers everything the
        -- cycle reconciles. Two NESTED feed epics whose subtrees overlap would
        -- still contend, since the subtree stale-delete reaches any child epic;
        -- not reachable today, and out of scope.
        --
        -- BOUNDED COST (was ACCEPTED COST until #4150) — a hung feed command
        -- starves its epic for at most feed_command_timeout (core.allium
        -- config), not forever.
        -- src/feed/exec.rs::exec_feed_command wraps the child in
        -- tokio::time::timeout(feed_command_timeout, ...), with
        -- kill_on_drop(true) set on the Command, so a command that never exits
        -- is killed and reaped at the deadline instead of awaited to
        -- completion. src/process.rs::run_bounded, the repo's kill-on-timeout
        -- primitive, only covers SYNCHRONOUS ProcessRunner children and so
        -- could not be reused for this async call site directly;
        -- tokio::time::timeout + kill_on_drop(true) is the async counterpart —
        -- dropping the timed-out future drops the Child, and kill_on_drop is
        -- what turns that drop into a kill-and-reap rather than an orphan.
        -- Because the claim still spans the exec, the epic's claim IS held for
        -- up to feed_command_timeout — every tick and every manual "r" for
        -- that epic in that window is still dropped, exactly as before — but
        -- the window is now bounded, and the epic recovers on its own once the
        -- deadline fires: no restart needed. This remains a real change from
        -- pre-#4091 behaviour (the two paths were unserialised then, so a
        -- manual refresh still worked while a poll was stuck), and the
        -- residual bounded window is deliberate for the same reason the
        -- original claim was: letting a manual refresh STEAL an aged claim
        -- would reintroduce exactly the interleave this rule exists to
        -- prevent. A future reader must not "fix" the residual window by
        -- loosening the claim; shrinking feed_command_timeout is the
        -- sanctioned lever if the window proves too wide in practice.
        --
        -- Coverage: src/feed/guard.rs unit tests (exclusivity per epic, release
        -- on drop, independence across epics, release when the holder panics);
        -- src/feed/mod.rs::tick_skips_an_epic_whose_cycle_is_already_in_flight
        -- and
        -- src/runtime/tests/feeds.rs::exec_trigger_epic_feed_reports_already_refreshing_while_a_cycle_is_in_flight
        -- (each request surface drops and writes nothing while a claim is
        -- held); and
        -- src/runtime/tests/feeds.rs::manual_refresh_is_dropped_while_a_real_auto_poll_cycle_is_in_flight
        -- (both surfaces against one epic with a genuine cycle mid-exec, which
        -- is the only test that would catch the two surfaces being wired to
        -- different registries); and
        -- src/feed/cycle.rs::a_hung_command_times_out_and_the_epic_recovers_on_the_next_cycle
        -- (the bounded-cost case: a hung command times out, fails, releases
        -- its claim, and a fresh cycle for the same epic afterward proceeds
        -- and syncs).
}

-- == Upsert semantics ==

rule UpsertFeedTasks {
    when: FeedCommandCompleted(epic, items)

    -- Items are matched against existing tasks by (epic_id, external_id).
    --
    -- == Repo path resolution ==
    --
    -- Before upserting, the runner resolves a repo_path for each item.
    -- The resolution does NOT consult the parent epic's repo_path —
    -- feed tasks live in whichever local clone matches the item's
    -- upstream URL, which can differ from epic to epic and item to
    -- item.
    --
    -- The resolution is, per item:
    --
    --   1. extract_github_repo(item.url) returns Some("org/repo") for
    --      well-formed GitHub URLs (https://github.com/org/repo/...)
    --      or None for empty, non-GitHub, or malformed URLs.
    --   2. resolve_repo_path("org/repo", known_paths) matches that
    --      pair against the local repo paths registered in the
    --      dispatch DB (db.list_repo_paths). Returns Some(local_path)
    --      on match, None otherwise.
    --   3. If either step yields None, the item's resolved path is the
    --      empty-string sentinel "". An empty repo_path is a valid
    --      stored state but blocks dispatch (see DispatchRequiresRepoPath).
    --
    -- If db.list_repo_paths fails the runner logs a warning and treats
    -- known_paths as empty, so every item resolves to "".
    --
    -- == Per-item upsert ==
    --
    --   - New (no match): a Task is inserted with the FeedItem's
    --     title, description, tag, labels, sort_order, wrap_up_mode (null
    --     when the item omits it), repo_path =
    --     resolved_path[item] (which may be ""), status = item.status,
    --     sub_status = SubStatus.default_for(item.status), external_id =
    --     item.external_id, base_branch = the resolved default branch
    --     of resolved_path[item] (read from `origin/HEAD`, falling
    --     back to "main" when resolved_path is empty or the ref is
    --     missing), epic = epic. Additionally, when item.url is non-empty,
    --     the inserted Task's url is set from item.url with url_type
    --     resolved by precedence: an explicit item.url_type wins; when
    --     absent the type is INFERRED from the URL string — a "/pull/"
    --     segment yields pr, an "/issues/" segment yields issue, and
    --     anything else yields other.
    --     A feed script that supplies a URL is asserting that the item
    --     points to a known artifact (typically a PR), so the card
    --     surfaces it immediately. When item.url is empty, url is null on
    --     insert and any declared url_type is ignored.
    --
    --   - Match: title, description, tag, labels, and sort_order on the
    --     existing Task are updated to the new values; status, sub_status,
    --     repo_path, and wrap_up_mode are NOT touched (wrap_up_mode is
    --     insert-only, so a user's manual wrap-up choice survives — but note
    --     an item that drops out of an emission and later reappears is
    --     deleted and reinserted, taking the feed's wrap_up_mode again, the
    --     same reappear-caveat as status/repo_path). url (and its url_type) are
    --     backfilled TOGETHER from item.url when the existing Task's url
    --     is null (COALESCE semantics), so feed tasks that pre-date the
    --     URL-on-insert behavior, or were inserted from a script revision
    --     that did not emit a URL, get their url populated on the next
    --     refresh once a URL is available. The backfilled url_type follows
    --     the same precedence as insert: explicit item.url_type wins,
    --     inference from the URL string otherwise. An existing non-null
    --     url (and its type) is never overwritten — a URL set by an agent
    --     or carried from the original insert wins over feed-supplied
    --     values.
    --     updated_at is
    --     bumped. labels and sort_order are feed-controlled (never
    --     user-editable in this iteration), so they are always replaced —
    --     EXCEPT when the existing task's status is done: the re-poll
    --     sort_order update is skipped in that case, so a completion-order
    --     value already on the task (set by ConfirmDone/PrMerged/
    --     ExitSession — PrClosed no longer transitions the task into done,
    --     so it doesn't set this — see tasks.allium and
    --     pr-workflow.allium) is never clobbered by the feed's severity-
    --     rank re-poll. Only newly-inserted tasks, or re-polls of a
    --     non-done task, take the feed's sort_order.
    --     This means a user who manually fixes the repo_path on a feed
    --     task (via 'e') keeps that correction across subsequent feed
    --     refreshes, while the feed-owned tag, labels, and sort_order
    --     stay in sync with the script output (subject to the done
    --     exception above).
    --
    -- Tasks present locally that are NOT in the latest emission are
    -- removed. The dispatch runtime treats the feed as the source of
    -- truth: any feed-managed task (external_id IS NOT NULL) that does
    -- not appear in the current emission is deleted from the epic.
    -- Non-feed tasks (external_id = null) in the same epic are never
    -- touched by this rule.
    --
    -- That source-of-truth reading holds only for an emission dispatch
    -- trusts, on a feed that mirrors state at all. An ADDITIVE cycle removes
    -- nothing: the per-item upsert above runs unchanged, the deletion below
    -- does not run at all. Two independent causes make a cycle additive —
    -- DegradedNonEmptyEmission (this emission is not trusted) and
    -- AppendOnlyFeed (this epic never mirrors) — and this clause cannot tell
    -- them apart, nor does it need to.

    ensures:
        let known_paths = list_repo_paths() ?? {}
        for item in items:
            let github_repo = extract_github_repo(item.url)
            let resolved =
                if github_repo != null:
                    resolve_repo_path(github_repo, known_paths) ?? ""
                else:
                    ""
            -- Declared type wins over inference; only consulted when
            -- item.url is non-empty.
            let resolved_url_type = item.url_type ?? infer_url_type(item.url)
            let existing = Task{epic: epic, external_id: item.external_id}
            if not exists existing:
                Task.created(
                    title: item.title,
                    description: item.description,
                    tag: item.tag,
                    labels: item.labels,
                    sort_order: item.sort_order,
                    wrap_up_mode: item.wrap_up_mode,
                    repo_path: resolved,
                    status: item.status,
                    sub_status: core/SubStatus.default_for(item.status),
                    external_id: item.external_id,
                    epic: epic,
                    base_branch: detect_default_branch(resolved),
                    -- url built from item.url whenever non-empty; null
                    -- otherwise. url_type precedence: explicit
                    -- item.url_type when declared, else inferred from the
                    -- string ("/pull/" -> pr, "/issues/" -> issue, else
                    -- other). See the prose above for rationale.
                    url:
                        if item.url != "":
                            core/TaskUrl{url: item.url, url_type: resolved_url_type}
                        else:
                            null
                )
            else:
                existing.title = item.title
                existing.description = item.description
                existing.tag = item.tag
                existing.labels = item.labels
                -- Re-poll sort_order update is skipped when the task is
                -- already done, so a completion-order value is never
                -- clobbered by the feed's severity-rank re-poll.
                if existing.status != done:
                    existing.sort_order = item.sort_order
                -- url backfilled from item.url when existing.url is null,
                -- with the same explicit-wins url_type precedence as
                -- insert; existing non-null url is preserved.
                if existing.url = null and item.url != "":
                    existing.url = core/TaskUrl{url: item.url, url_type: resolved_url_type}
                -- existing.status, existing.sub_status, existing.repo_path,
                -- existing.wrap_up_mode preserved

        -- Feed-as-source-of-truth deletion. Removing a feed task is not a
        -- bare row drop: the row is deleted and reported, and the reported
        -- row then gets tasks.allium's TaskTeardown — tmux window killed,
        -- git worktree removed, best-effort branch deletion — exactly the
        -- teardown ArchiveTask and DeleteTask perform. A stale feed task
        -- can carry an in-flight agent's session, and dropping the row
        -- alone orphans that worktree on disk.
        --
        -- SKIPPED ENTIRELY when the cycle is additive, for either reason.
        -- Under DegradedNonEmptyEmission, absence from a tainted emission is
        -- not evidence of anything. Under AppendOnlyFeed, absence from ANY
        -- emission is not evidence of anything, because the source emits
        -- events that are never retracted. Either way this clause is where
        -- that missing evidence would be acted on irreversibly.
        if not additive:
            for task in Tasks where epic = epic and external_id != null:
                if not items.any(i => i.external_id = task.external_id):
                    not exists task
                    TaskTeardown(task)

    @guidance
        -- Status preservation is intentional: once a user starts working
        -- on a feed-derived task (moves it to running, opens a PR, etc.),
        -- subsequent feed refreshes must not yank the card back to its
        -- original column.
        -- repo_path preservation on conflict is for the same reason: a
        -- user-corrected path must survive feed refreshes. Only the
        -- INSERT path writes a repo_path; the UPDATE path leaves it
        -- alone.
        -- The ON CONFLICT clause is conditional on external_id NOT NULL —
        -- non-feed tasks (external_id = null) never collide.
        -- Implementation: src/db/queries/tasks.rs::upsert_feed_tasks (takes a
        -- repo_paths slice, one entry per item, parallel to items, plus a
        -- parallel base_branches slice). The parallel-slice contract is
        -- enforced up front: if items, repo_paths, and base_branches do not all
        -- share the same length the call errors and writes nothing, rather than
        -- letting the index-zip silently truncate and drop items. This is the
        -- ONE place in the feed pipeline the three collections are still
        -- parallel slices — every caller above it (sync_grouped_feed,
        -- run_feed_sync, run_role_routed_feed_sync) instead threads a single
        -- Vec<src/feed/ingest/mod.rs::FeedItemWithTarget> of paired
        -- {item, repo_path, base_branch} entries, assembled once via
        -- FeedItemWithTarget::zip at the FeedCommandCompleted boundary
        -- (FeedRunner::tick, exec_trigger_epic_feed), so per-index alignment
        -- is structural end-to-end and needs no separate length check.
        -- Resolution helpers: src/dispatch/mod.rs::extract_github_repo,
        -- src/dispatch/mod.rs::resolve_repo_path. Callers:
        -- src/feed/ingest/mod.rs::run_feed_sync (via FeedRunner.tick and exec_trigger_epic_feed).
        -- The TaskTeardown obligation on the stale-delete is met by reporting,
        -- not by tearing down inline: upsert_feed_tasks' delete carries
        -- RETURNING id, repo_path, worktree, tmux_window and yields the removed
        -- rows that still owned a worktree or a tmux window as
        -- src/db/mod.rs::RemovedFeedTask values (rows owning neither need no
        -- teardown and are not reported; the delete itself is unconditional).
        -- They ride out on src/feed/ingest/mod.rs::FeedSyncOutcome and the two
        -- feed entry points pass them to
        -- src/feed/mod.rs::cleanup_removed_feed_tasks — see the TaskTeardown
        -- fan-out paragraph under RoleRoutedFeedSync for the whole path.
}

rule GroupedFeedUpsert {
    when: FeedCommandCompleted(epic, items)

    requires: epic.group_by_repo = true

    -- When group_by_repo is enabled, items are grouped by repo name
    -- (last path segment of extract_github_repo(url)), with "other" as the
    -- fallback for items that lack a resolvable GitHub URL.
    -- A sub-epic is found or created for each group name, then
    -- UpsertFeedTasks is called per sub-epic. Active sub-epics that the
    -- current emission no longer names (their repo contributed no item) are
    -- reconciled too: UpsertFeedTasks is called on each with an empty list,
    -- so the feed-as-source-of-truth deletion in UpsertFeedTasks extends
    -- across the whole grouped subtree rather than only the present repos.
    -- Finally UpsertFeedTasks is called on the parent with an empty list,
    -- which the same deletion rule uses to remove all flat feed tasks from
    -- the parent.
    -- This doubles as migration: on the first run after the flag is enabled,
    -- pre-existing flat tasks are deleted and recreated under their repo sub-epics.
    --
    -- Every deletion reached through those UpsertFeedTasks calls — in a present
    -- group, in an absent sub-epic, or in the parent's empty upsert — deletes
    -- the row, reports it, and then performs tasks.allium's TaskTeardown on the
    -- reported row: tmux window killed, git worktree removed, best-effort
    -- branch deletion.
    -- Delete-then-teardown, not teardown-then-delete: the delete and the report
    -- are one DELETE ... RETURNING statement, so what gets torn down is exactly
    -- what was deleted, with no SELECT-then-DELETE window in which the two can
    -- disagree. The cost is that a crash between the commit and the teardown
    -- orphans the worktree with no DB row left to retry from (see the sequencing
    -- note under RoleRoutedFeedSync). Grouping migration in
    -- particular deletes and recreates tasks wholesale, so a feed task carrying
    -- an in-flight agent must not have its worktree left orphaned on disk.
    --
    -- On an ADDITIVE cycle the present groups are
    -- still upserted, but BOTH clearing passes — absent sub-epics and the
    -- parent's flat tasks — are skipped, so nothing is removed and no
    -- TaskTeardown runs. Note that grouping migration is a clearing pass in
    -- disguise (it deletes flat tasks and recreates them under sub-epics), so
    -- an additive cycle also defers the migration to the next trusted
    -- emission rather than half-performing it.
    -- Only ONE of the two causes of additivity reaches this rule:
    -- DegradedNonEmptyEmission, for which deferring the migration is right.
    -- AppendOnlyFeed cannot — a grouped epic may not be append-only
    -- (AppendOnlyFeed), so there is no configuration in which the deferral
    -- here is permanent.

    ensures:
        let groups = group_by(items, item => repo_name(item.url))
        for (name, group_items) in groups:
            let sub_epic = Epic.find(parent = epic, title = name)
                           ?? Epic.created(title: name, parent: epic,
                                           project_id: epic.project_id,
                                           repo_path: "", description: "")
            UpsertFeedTasks(sub_epic, group_items)
        -- Reconcile sub-epics absent from this emission. An active sub-epic
        -- whose title no current item maps to has its feed tasks cleared
        -- (manual tasks survive, per UpsertFeedTasks). When items is empty,
        -- every active sub-epic is cleared. Skipped entirely when additive.
        if not additive:
            for sub_epic in epic.active_sub_epics:
                if not items.any(item => repo_name(item.url) = sub_epic.title):
                    UpsertFeedTasks(sub_epic, {})
            FeedCommandCompleted(epic, {})

    @guidance
        -- repo_name(url) = last path segment of extract_github_repo(url),
        -- or "other" when the URL yields no GitHub repo (empty URL, non-GitHub,
        -- malformed). Implementation: src/feed/ingest/grouped.rs::sync_grouped_feed,
        -- src/models/paths.rs::repo_name_from_url.
        -- Sub-epics are not auto-deleted when their tasks disappear; empty
        -- sub-epics remain for the user to clean up manually. The
        -- reconciliation only clears their feed tasks — it never removes the
        -- sub-epic row itself.
        -- UpsertFeedTasks(epic, []) uses the existing deletion rule to remove
        -- all feed-managed tasks (external_id IS NOT NULL) directly under the
        -- parent epic; the same call on an absent sub-epic clears that
        -- sub-epic's feed tasks while preserving any manual (external_id =
        -- null) tasks the user added there.
        -- Implementation: src/feed/ingest/grouped.rs::sync_grouped_feed — the present
        -- groups and the absent-sub-epic reconciliation share the same
        -- upsert_feed_tasks path, and therefore the same removed-row report:
        -- sync_grouped_feed returns the affected sub-epic ids alongside the
        -- concatenated src/db/mod.rs::RemovedFeedTask rows from every one of
        -- those calls, which reach src/feed/mod.rs::cleanup_removed_feed_tasks
        -- via src/feed/ingest/mod.rs::FeedSyncOutcome. Grouping migration
        -- deletes and recreates wholesale, so this is the path where an
        -- unreported removal would orphan the most worktrees.
}

-- == Flat reconcile (un-grouped feed epic) ==

rule FlatFeedReconcile {
    when: FeedCommandCompleted(epic, items)

    requires: epic.group_by_repo = false
    requires: epic.feed_role != reviews_parent

    -- The OFF-side counterpart to GroupedFeedUpsert's ON-side migration. When
    -- grouping is turned OFF on a feed epic, the flag flip is synchronous
    -- (epics.allium: ToggleGroupByRepo) but does NOT itself move any task.
    -- Reconciliation is deferred to the NEXT feed sync — this rule, the flat
    -- dispatch branch (see the FeedSync dispatch-precedence block below).
    --
    -- Before the flat upsert, every ACTIVE RepoGroup sub-epic of the parent
    -- (origin = repo_group, status != archived — the same set the manual
    -- FlattenEpic rule acts on, epics.allium) is reconciled: its tasks are
    -- re-homed back to the parent, and a sub-epic that ends up empty (no tasks,
    -- no child epics) is deleted. This mirrors FlattenEpic's semantics; the
    -- implementation reuses the same src/service/grouping.rs::flatten_epic
    -- helper rather than duplicating the re-home/delete-if-empty logic. Unlike
    -- the FlattenEpic RULE (which gates on feed_command = null and so never
    -- fires for a feed epic), this rule is the feed epic's route to that same
    -- outcome — poll-driven, not keypress-driven.
    --
    -- Manual (origin != repo_group) sub-epics are never touched, exactly as in
    -- FlattenEpic. reviews_parent epics are excluded: their whole subtree is
    -- reconciled by RoleRoutedFeedSync, which owns their sub-epic layout.
    --
    -- After re-homing, UpsertFeedTasks runs on the parent with the current
    -- emission. Re-homed feed tasks (external_id != null) are now direct
    -- children of the parent, so UpsertFeedTasks matches them by
    -- (epic, external_id) and either refreshes them (still emitted) or removes
    -- them as stale (absent from the emission) — the ordinary
    -- feed-as-source-of-truth behaviour. Removing such a stale task deletes and
    -- reports the row and then performs tasks.allium's TaskTeardown on the
    -- reported row — tmux window killed, git worktree removed, best-effort
    -- branch deletion — so a re-homed task that still
    -- carries an in-flight agent is released rather than orphaned. This is what
    -- makes the flat path self-healing on the first poll after the user
    -- un-groups, with no manual cleanup step. Running it when the parent has no RepoGroup sub-epics is a
    -- harmless no-op (empty loop), so it fires on every flat sync, not only the
    -- first after the toggle.
    --
    -- On an ADDITIVE cycle (DegradedNonEmptyEmission or AppendOnlyFeed) the re-home still runs —
    -- it moves tasks and deletes only sub-epics it has just emptied, so it
    -- destroys nothing and does not read the emission — while the removal half
    -- is skipped by UpsertFeedTasks' own additive gate. A re-homed task absent
    -- from a tainted emission therefore lands on the parent and STAYS there
    -- until a trusted emission judges it.

    ensures:
        for sub in epic.sub_epics where sub.origin = repo_group and sub.status != archived:
            for task in sub.subtasks: task.epic = epic
            -- sub.subtasks.count is the post-move count: true only when the
            -- sub-epic had no nested sub-epics and all its tasks just moved out.
            if sub.subtasks.count = 0 and sub.sub_epics.count = 0: DeleteEpic(sub)
        UpsertFeedTasks(epic, items)

    @guidance
        -- Implementation: src/feed/ingest/mod.rs::run_feed_sync flat branch
        -- (group_by_repo = false, feed_role != reviews_parent). The re-home +
        -- delete-if-empty step calls src/service/grouping.rs::flatten_epic
        -- (shared with the manual FlattenEpic path); confirm no caller relies on
        -- flatten_epic being non-feed-epic-only before reusing it. Symmetric
        -- with GroupedFeedUpsert's ON-side "doubles as migration" behaviour.
        -- The flat branch's upsert_feed_tasks call reports its removed rows in
        -- the same SHAPE as every other path (see UpsertFeedTasks' guidance),
        -- so a re-homed task deleted as stale is torn down rather than
        -- orphaned. Its FAILURE handling differs, though: the flat branch
        -- propagates with `?` and aborts the whole sync, where the grouped and
        -- role-routed paths funnel every write through
        -- src/feed/mod.rs::removed_or_warn and continue past a failed write
        -- with an empty report. One epic, one statement — there is no rest of
        -- the pass to salvage here, which is why the flat path can afford to
        -- be stricter.
        -- Coverage: src/feed/ingest/tests.rs (re-home + delete empty RepoGroup
        -- sub-epic on flat sync; no-op when no sub-epics exist; manual sub-epics
        -- preserved; reviews_parent epic unaffected — routed to
        -- RoleRoutedFeedSync instead);
        -- src/feed/ingest/tests.rs::flat_sync_reports_removed_task_with_worktree.
}

-- == Role-routed reconcile (PR-review feed) ==

rule RoleRoutedFeedSync {
    when: FeedCommandCompleted(epic, items)

    requires: epic.feed_role = reviews_parent

    -- The opinionated path (see the DELIBERATE EXCEPTION in Scope). A
    -- reviews_parent epic carries the reviews feed_command and ONE deduped
    -- emission covering every PR the user is involved with (see
    -- feed-scripts.allium: scripts/fetch-reviews.sh). This rule reconciles the
    -- parent's WHOLE role-sub-epic subtree — my_reviews, team_reviews, bots —
    -- from that single emission, rather than reconciling any epic against its
    -- own emission.
    --
    -- The three role sub-epics are ensured to exist up front (idempotent,
    -- matched by core/Epic.feed_role, never by title). They carry NO
    -- feed_command, so FeedTick never polls them independently (enforced at
    -- provisioning — see epics.allium ProvisionManagedEpics); only the parent
    -- is polled, and only this rule writes into the subtree.
    --
    -- == Own-authored PRs are excluded before routing ==
    --
    -- The emission is FILTERED before anything else happens to it: every item
    -- carrying author_me is dropped (the ExcludeOwnAuthored contract above).
    -- The kept set is what the routing loop, the insert/update pass and the
    -- stale keep-set all see, so an excluded item is not merely routed
    -- elsewhere — it does not exist for this reconcile.
    --
    -- The consequence for an item that WAS present is deliberate: because it
    -- is absent from the keep-set, the subtree delete removes its task exactly
    -- as it removes a merged PR, TaskTeardown included. So enabling this
    -- exclusion cleans out own-authored PRs already sitting in the subtree on
    -- the next poll, rather than leaving them stranded until they merge. A
    -- MANUAL task (external_id = null) is never touched, so a task the user
    -- created for their own PR by hand — or a dispatch task that opened it —
    -- is unaffected.
    --
    -- The filter runs on the additive (DegradedNonEmptyEmission or
    -- AppendOnlyFeed) path too: it
    -- shrinks what gets inserted, and the deletes it would otherwise imply are
    -- skipped along with every other removal on that path.
    --
    -- == Global identity and the move ==
    --
    -- Task identity across the subtree is GLOBAL by external_id (NOT
    -- (epic_id, external_id) as in UpsertFeedTasks): the same PR is one task no
    -- matter which role sub-epic currently holds it. The reconcile SCOPE is the
    -- whole reviews_parent subtree INCLUDING the parent epic itself — a feed
    -- task found sitting directly on the parent is in scope exactly like one in
    -- a role sub-epic. This is deliberate: a reviews_parent epic must hold NO
    -- feed-managed task directly (see NoFlatFeedTasksOnReviewsParent). A flat
    -- feed task can be stranded on the parent by an out-of-band flat upsert
    -- (e.g. an older binary, or a manual trigger that pre-dates feed_role
    -- dispatch); this rule is what heals it. For each emitted PR,
    -- route(item.signals) (the RouteSignals contract) picks the target role:
    --
    --   - Present in the subtree, SAME role  -> fields updated in place
    --     (title, description, tag, labels, sort_order), as UpsertFeedTasks.
    --   - Present in the subtree, DIFFERENT epic -> a MOVE: the task's epic_id
    --     is reassigned to the target sub-epic, THEN its fields are updated.
    --     "Different epic" INCLUDES a task currently on the parent — it is
    --     moved down into its routed role sub-epic, not duplicate-inserted.
    --     The reassignment touches only epic_id/updated_at, so status,
    --     sub_status, repo_path, worktree, tmux_window and sort_order are
    --     PRESERVED — an in-flight dispatched review agent keeps its session
    --     across a my<->team<->bots reclassification or a parent->role rescue.
    --     This is the core fix: a bucket change is a true move, not
    --     delete+recreate.
    --   - Absent from the subtree -> inserted into the target sub-epic (fresh
    --     task, exactly as UpsertFeedTasks' insert path).
    --
    -- Because a parent-stranded task is MOVED (not re-inserted), it never
    -- collides with itself under the subtree-uniqueness constraint
    -- (enforce_feed_task_subtree_unique_insert): the move relocates the single
    -- existing row rather than inserting a second one. Treating the parent as
    -- out of scope — so the router insert-inserts a routed copy while the flat
    -- copy lingers — is precisely the deadlock this scoping avoids.
    --
    -- == Sub-epic-level grouping (a THIRD tree level) ==
    --
    -- The routing above places a PR in its role sub-epic (my/team/bots). When
    -- that ROLE SUB-EPIC itself has group_by_repo enabled (can_auto_group on
    -- the sub-epic — src/models/epics.rs), the PR is routed ONE LEVEL DEEPER:
    -- into a per-repo REPO-GROUP sub-epic nested UNDER the role sub-epic,
    -- found-or-created and keyed by repo name
    -- (src/models/paths.rs::repo_name_from_url of item.url).
    -- Tasks then live at this third level, not
    -- directly on the role sub-epic. When the role sub-epic does NOT have
    -- grouping enabled the target is the role sub-epic directly — today's
    -- documented two-level behaviour is the group_by_repo = false case.
    --
    -- This is the same group_by_repo grouping GroupedFeedUpsert applies at the
    -- top level of a flat feed epic, but here it is applied to an ALREADY
    -- role-routed sub-epic, so it produces a THIRD tree level
    -- (parent -> role sub-epic -> repo-group sub-epic -> tasks) rather than a
    -- second. A per-sync cache keyed by (role_sub_epic_id, repo_name) memoises
    -- the found-or-created repo-group id for the duration of ONE reconcile, so
    -- multiple items sharing a repo do not create duplicate sub-epics. If the
    -- repo-group sub-epic cannot be created, the item falls back to the role
    -- sub-epic directly (logged, not fatal).
    --
    -- The third level widens every subtree operation below (identity lookup,
    -- stale delete, recalculation) — see each section for the specifics.
    --
    -- == Subtree-scoped delete (the reason this is not UpsertFeedTasks) ==
    --
    -- UpsertFeedTasks' delete pass is PER-EPIC: it removes feed tasks absent
    -- from THAT epic's emission. Run per-role that would delete a task the
    -- moment it moved to another role (it is absent from the losing role's
    -- keep-set). So the stale-delete here is SUBTREE-SCOPED and uses a SINGLE
    -- union keep-set of every emitted external_id: a task that merely moved is
    -- still in the keep-set and survives, while a PR absent from the whole
    -- emission (merged or closed) is removed. Because the delete SQL is one
    -- level deep, that single keep-set is applied with the PARENT as root
    -- (reaching the role sub-epics' tasks) AND once per ROLE SUB-EPIC as root
    -- (reaching that role's repo-group grandchildren — the third level above).
    -- The role-level pass runs ALWAYS, regardless of whether that role
    -- currently has group_by_repo enabled, so repo-group tasks orphaned after
    -- grouping is toggled off are still cleaned up. Manual tasks
    -- (external_id = null) anywhere in the subtree are never touched, as in
    -- UpsertFeedTasks. Roles that contributed no item this cycle are cleared by
    -- the same delete (no per-role empty-upsert needed).
    --
    -- == Parent sweep ==
    --
    -- The subtree delete above is scoped to the parent's CHILDREN, so it never
    -- touches tasks flat on the parent itself. A final parent sweep therefore
    -- removes every feed-managed task (external_id != null) still sitting
    -- directly on the reviews_parent epic. By construction a present
    -- parent-stranded task was already MOVED down by the routing loop, so what
    -- remains is a merged/closed stray or a legacy duplicate whose routed copy
    -- already lives in a role sub-epic — both must go so the parent holds no
    -- flat feed task (NoFlatFeedTasksOnReviewsParent). Manual (external_id =
    -- null) tasks on the parent are preserved. This converges the invariant from
    -- any starting state, including the corrupt parent+sub-epic duplicate state
    -- the old flat-upsert bug produced.
    --
    -- Steps run as ONE non-interleaved unit per parent tick. Because the whole
    -- emission is reconciled together, "present but different role" = move and
    -- "absent" = remove are unambiguous — the property independent per-epic
    -- feeds could not provide.

    ensures:
        -- Ensure the three role sub-epics exist (idempotent, by feed_role).
        -- role_sub_epic(parent, role) finds-or-creates the single sub-epic of
        -- the parent carrying that feed_role (uniqueness guaranteed by
        -- core/Epic.UniqueFeedRolePerParent).
        let my   = role_sub_epic(epic, my_reviews)
        let team = role_sub_epic(epic, team_reviews)
        let bots = role_sub_epic(epic, bots)

        -- Drop own-authored items up front (ExcludeOwnAuthored). `kept` — not
        -- `items` — is what the rest of this rule reconciles against, INCLUDING
        -- the stale-delete keep-set below, so an excluded item that already had
        -- a task loses it on this cycle.
        let kept = items.filter(i => not excluded(i.signals))

        for item in kept:
            -- route only ever yields my_reviews / team_reviews / bots. It is
            -- never reached for an excluded item.
            let role = route(item.signals)
            let role_target =
                if role = team_reviews: team
                else if role = bots:    bots
                else:                   my
            -- Third tree level: when the role sub-epic itself has grouping
            -- enabled, route one level deeper into a per-repo sub-epic nested
            -- under it (found-or-created, keyed by repo name, memoised per
            -- sync). Otherwise the target is the role sub-epic directly.
            let target =
                if role_target.can_auto_group:
                    repo_group_sub_epic(role_target, repo_name(item.url))
                else:
                    role_target
            -- Scope: the whole reviews_parent subtree, PARENT INCLUDED, across
            -- ALL THREE levels (parent, role sub-epics, repo-group
            -- grandchildren). A task stranded directly on the parent
            -- (existing.epic = epic) is found here too, so it is moved down
            -- rather than duplicate-inserted. Identity is global by
            -- external_id; the scan order (parent, then repo-group
            -- grandchildren, then role sub-epics last) makes a
            -- role-sub-epic-direct copy win a tie over a repo-group copy.
            let existing = Task{external_id: item.external_id}
                           where existing.epic = epic
                              or existing.epic.parent_epic_id = epic.id
                              or existing.epic.parent_epic_id in {my.id, team.id, bots.id}
            if exists existing:
                if existing.epic != target:
                    -- MOVE: reassign epic only; state-bearing fields preserved.
                    -- Covers parent->role rescue as well as my<->team<->bots.
                    existing.epic = target
                -- Field refresh (same as UpsertFeedTasks' match path).
                existing.title = item.title
                existing.description = item.description
                existing.tag = item.tag
                existing.labels = item.labels
                existing.sort_order = item.sort_order
                -- existing.status, sub_status, repo_path, worktree,
                -- tmux_window preserved by the move.
            else:
                -- First sight: insert into the routed sub-epic exactly as
                -- UpsertFeedTasks' insert path (repo_path/base_branch/url all
                -- resolved identically).
                UpsertFeedTasks(target, {item})

        -- Subtree-scoped delete with the union of all emitted ids: removes
        -- merged/closed PRs and clears buckets absent from this emission,
        -- without deleting a just-moved task (it is in the keep-set) or any
        -- manual task. The delete SQL is one level deep, so the union keep-set
        -- is applied with the PARENT as root (role sub-epics' tasks) AND once
        -- per ROLE SUB-EPIC as root (repo-group grandchildren). The role-level
        -- pass runs ALWAYS, even for roles with grouping off, so orphaned
        -- repo-group tasks are cleaned up. Both levels are captured here by
        -- matching any feed task whose epic is a child of the parent OR a child
        -- of a role sub-epic. Removal deletes the row, reports it, and then
        -- performs tasks.allium's TaskTeardown on the reported row — this is
        -- the path a merged PR's in-flight review agent travels, so its tmux
        -- window and worktree must be released, not orphaned. Skipped
        -- entirely when the cycle is additive (DegradedNonEmptyEmission or
        -- AppendOnlyFeed): this
        -- is the exact clause that would destroy a live review agent's
        -- worktree on a partially degraded emission.
        if not additive:
            for task in Tasks where (task.epic.parent_epic_id = epic.id
                                     or task.epic.parent_epic_id in {my.id, team.id, bots.id})
                                and task.external_id != null:
                if not kept.any(i => i.external_id = task.external_id):
                    not exists task
                    TaskTeardown(task)

        -- Parent sweep: the reviews_parent epic must hold NO feed task directly
        -- (NoFlatFeedTasksOnReviewsParent). Present parent-stranded tasks were
        -- already moved down by the routing loop, so anything left flat on the
        -- parent is a merged/closed stray or a legacy duplicate whose routed
        -- copy already lives in a role sub-epic — both are removed. Manual
        -- (external_id = null) tasks on the parent are preserved. Removal
        -- deletes-then-tears-down the reported row, as above. Skipped when
        -- additive: this sweep deletes on position alone, with no reference to
        -- the emission at all, so on a tainted cycle it is the least
        -- discriminating delete of the three and the first to go.
        if not additive:
            for task in Tasks where task.epic = epic and task.external_id != null:
                not exists task
                TaskTeardown(task)

        -- Recalculate bottom-up through all three levels: repo-group sub-epics
        -- first (they roll up into their role sub-epic), then each role
        -- sub-epic (it may have lost tasks to a move or the delete), then the
        -- parent.
        for rg in repo_group_sub_epics:
            RecalculateEpicStatus(rg)
        for sub in {my, team, bots}:
            RecalculateEpicStatus(sub)
        RecalculateEpicStatus(epic)

    @guidance
        -- Implementation: src/feed/ingest/role_routed.rs::run_role_routed_feed_sync,
        -- selected by the shared dispatcher
        -- src/feed/ingest/mod.rs::run_feed_sync_by_role when epic.feed_role =
        -- ReviewsParent (a MyReviews/TeamReviews/Bots epic with a feed_command is
        -- a provisioning bug, rejected by the provisioning guard in
        -- src/feed/cycle.rs::FeedCycle::run (the debug_assert! block), which
        -- debug_asserts and fails the cycle; that guard does NOT itself do the
        -- role->path selection. Because it sits in the shared cycle it now
        -- covers the manual "r" refresh too, which previously had no such
        -- guard and would have flat-upserted into the role sub-epic).
        -- Third-level (repo-group) routing lives in
        -- src/feed/ingest/routing.rs::route_and_group_entries: per item, after
        -- roles.target_for(route(signals)) gives the role sub-epic
        -- (role_target), if roles.can_auto_group(role_target) is true the
        -- actual target is a repo-group sub-epic found-or-created via
        -- db.create_repo_group_sub_epic (src/db/mod.rs), keyed by
        -- src/models/paths.rs::repo_name_from_url and memoised in an
        -- in-memory (role_sub_epic_id, repo_name) -> repo_group_epic_id cache
        -- for the duration of one sync; a create failure falls back to
        -- role_target. can_auto_group is src/models/epics.rs::can_auto_group (group_by_repo
        -- && feed_command.is_none()).
        -- The subtree delete is
        -- src/db/queries/tasks.rs::delete_stale_subtree_feed_tasks, ONE level
        -- deep (DELETE ... WHERE epic_id IN (SELECT id FROM epics WHERE
        -- parent_epic_id = ?1) ... RETURNING id, repo_path, worktree,
        -- tmux_window). src/feed/ingest/stale.rs::delete_stale_subtree
        -- therefore calls it once with the parent as root (reaches role
        -- sub-epics) AND once per role sub-epic as root (reaches repo-group
        -- grandchildren) — always, regardless of that role's grouping flag. The
        -- parent itself is cleared separately by the parent sweep
        -- (src/feed/ingest/stale.rs::clear_parent_stranded_tasks, an empty
        -- upsert_feed_tasks — whose per-epic stale-delete carries the same
        -- RETURNING clause).
        --
        -- TaskTeardown fan-out. Both deletes report what they removed, so the
        -- row does not vanish silently and orphan an in-flight agent's
        -- worktree. The shared row type is src/db/mod.rs::RemovedFeedTask
        -- {id, repo_path, worktree, tmux_window}; only rows that still own a
        -- worktree or a tmux window are reported (the RETURNING statement
        -- deletes every stale row either way — reporting only narrows what the
        -- caller has to clean up). src/feed/mod.rs::removed_or_warn is the
        -- log-and-discard wrapper that keeps the Ok payload, so a failed write
        -- contributes an empty vec rather than aborting the pass.
        -- src/feed/ingest/role_routed.rs::run_role_routed_feed_sync
        -- concatenates the reports from src/feed/ingest/upsert.rs::upsert_role_groups,
        -- delete_stale_subtree and clear_parent_stranded_tasks — IN THAT ORDER,
        -- which is load-bearing: each statement filters on the task's CURRENT
        -- epic_id, so the move must land before the deletes or a MOVED task is
        -- reported as removed and has its live worktree destroyed. The
        -- concatenation rides out on the `removed` half of
        -- src/feed/ingest/mod.rs::FeedSyncOutcome, which
        -- src/feed/cycle.rs::FeedCycle::run hands to
        -- src/feed/mod.rs::cleanup_removed_feed_tasks -- once, in the shared
        -- cycle, rather than once per feed path as when teardown was first
        -- added. It groups
        -- by repo_path and tears down sequentially within a repo (git worktree
        -- remove / branch -D contend on the shared checkout's index lock) and
        -- in parallel across repos. Teardown is best-effort and warn-logged;
        -- it never fails the sync. It is also unconditional on task state —
        -- no clause here consults status or sub_status, so any task that
        -- reaches it is force-torn-down even with a live agent in it. That is
        -- deliberate and stays: the protection against tearing down a live
        -- agent lives UPSTREAM, in whether the delete happens at all
        -- (DegradedNonEmptyEmission, or AppendOnlyFeed for an epic that
        -- never removes at all), not in a state check here. See the former
        -- rule's "why not gate the teardown on task state instead" for the
        -- alternative that was rejected, and do not reintroduce it here.
        --
        -- Sequencing note — the ensures clauses of this rule and of
        -- UpsertFeedTasks / GroupedFeedUpsert write `not exists task` BEFORE
        -- TaskTeardown(task), which is the order the implementation runs in:
        -- the DELETE ... RETURNING commits first and the teardown runs
        -- afterwards in the caller, off FeedSyncOutcome.removed. The delete
        -- and the report are the SAME statement on purpose — a
        -- SELECT-then-DELETE pair would leave a window in which the set torn
        -- down and the set deleted disagree (a row could be modified, moved,
        -- or deleted between the two), and RETURNING closes it.
        -- The cost, stated honestly: if the process dies between the commit
        -- and the teardown, the worktree is orphaned on disk with no row left
        -- to retry from. That is accepted — the alternative is holding a write
        -- transaction open across `git worktree remove` — and it is bounded,
        -- since the orphan is an inert directory under .worktrees/, not a
        -- resource leak.
        --
        -- Teardown-vs-notification ordering is NORMATIVE and the same on both
        -- feed paths: the teardown of every removed row completes BEFORE the
        -- notification for that cycle is sent. So a notification means
        -- reconciled AND cleaned up, and the board never shows a row gone while
        -- its worktree is still being removed.
        --
        -- This resolves what was previously an unspecified divergence — the
        -- auto-poll path notified first, the manual path cleaned up first — and
        -- it resolves it towards the manual path's stronger guarantee. It became
        -- a single decision to make rather than two behaviours to keep in step
        -- once both paths were funnelled through the one shared cycle
        -- (src/feed/cycle.rs::FeedCycle::run, which owns the teardown and hands
        -- its callers an already-cleaned-up outcome to present).
        --
        -- The cost, stated honestly: the auto-poll path's McpEvent::EpicChanged
        -- now waits on `git worktree remove` for every removed task, so a
        -- mass-merge that removes a dozen tasks at once lags the board by that
        -- fan-out. It is bounded — cleanup_removed_feed_tasks runs repos in
        -- parallel and only serialises within a repo — and buying it is the
        -- elimination of a window in which the board disagreed with the
        -- filesystem. The previous asymmetry was not a considered trade; it was
        -- an artefact of two call sites growing the same feature independently.
        --
        -- The existing-task lookup
        -- that decides
        -- move-vs-insert is
        -- src/feed/ingest/role_routed.rs::build_existing_task_index: it
        -- scans the parent FIRST (lowest priority), then each role sub-epic's
        -- children (repo-group grandchildren), then the role sub-epics
        -- themselves LAST — so external_id identity is global across all three
        -- levels and a role-sub-epic-direct copy wins ties over a repo-group
        -- copy. A present parent-stranded task is thus moved (single-row
        -- relocation, no uniqueness-trigger collision, in-flight state
        -- preserved) before the sweep runs. The move is
        -- db.set_task_epic_id (epic_id +
        -- updated_at only) THEN db.patch_task(fields). role_sub_epic is
        -- ensure_role_sub_epic (idempotent, matched by feed_role). Recalculation
        -- is src/feed/ingest/role_routed.rs::recalculate_subtree: repo-group
        -- sub-epics first (bottom-up), then role sub-epics, then the parent.
        -- Coverage:
        -- src/feed/ingest/tests.rs (move preserves in-flight worktree/status;
        -- moved task not deleted same cycle; insert on first sight; delete on
        -- merge/close; manual-task preservation; multi-role single emission;
        -- two back-to-back zero-interval ticks lose nothing; a feed task
        -- stranded on the parent is moved down to its routed role sub-epic with
        -- state preserved; a parent-stranded task absent from the emission is
        -- deleted as stale; a manual task on the parent is never touched);
        -- src/feed/ingest/tests.rs::moved_task_is_never_reported_as_removed
        -- (the ordering invariant above — a task moved between role sub-epics
        -- is never handed to teardown),
        -- src/feed/ingest/tests.rs::absent_task_with_worktree_is_reported_as_removed
        -- and src/feed/ingest/tests.rs::flat_sync_reports_removed_task_with_worktree
        -- (the report reaches the outcome on both sync paths);
        -- src/db/tests/tasks.rs::delete_stale_subtree_feed_tasks_returns_removed_rows_with_state
        -- and src/db/tests/tasks.rs::upsert_feed_tasks_returns_removed_rows_with_state
        -- (the RETURNING contract on both stale-delete statements);
        -- src/feed/mod.rs::tick_removed_task_tears_down_its_worktree
        -- and src/runtime/tests/feeds.rs::exec_trigger_epic_feed_removed_task_tears_down_its_worktree
        -- (the teardown is actually wired up, once per feed path); and the
        -- src/feed/mod.rs cleanup_* tests for the helper itself
        -- (cleanup_removes_worktree_and_kills_window,
        -- cleanup_kills_window_only_when_there_is_no_worktree,
        -- cleanup_of_a_stateless_row_runs_no_commands,
        -- cleanup_continues_after_a_failure,
        -- cleanup_serialises_same_repo_removals).
        --
        -- Known limitation — GitHub search lag. GitHub's search API is
        -- eventually consistent: a just-reviewed PR can still match
        -- review-requested:@me for a poll cycle or two, so a role move may lag
        -- the real-world action briefly. Routing is correct once signals
        -- settle; this is documented, not a bug.
}

-- == Dispatch guard ==

rule DispatchRequiresRepoPath {
    when: DispatchAttempted(task)

    -- Feed tasks may end up with repo_path = "" when the runner cannot
    -- resolve the FeedItem URL to a known local clone. An empty
    -- repo_path is a valid stored state — the task remains visible on
    -- the kanban board and can be edited — but dispatch is refused
    -- because there is no worktree to create.
    --
    -- The user resolves this by pressing 'e' on the task and entering
    -- a repo_path manually, after which dispatch proceeds normally.
    -- This guard applies to every task, not just feed tasks; non-feed
    -- tasks ordinarily acquire a non-empty repo_path at creation time
    -- and so never trigger it.

    requires: task.repo_path != ""

    ensures: DispatchProceeded(task: task)

    @guidance
        -- Implementation: src/dispatch/agents.rs::dispatch_with_prompt
        -- returns an error before spawning any worktree or tmux window
        -- when task.repo_path.is_empty().
}

-- == Failure handling ==

rule FeedCommandFailure {
    when: FeedCommandFailed(epic, error)

    -- Failures fall into seven buckets, all handled identically from the
    -- user's perspective: the failure is logged at warn level and the
    -- epic's last_run is bumped so we don't retry until the next interval.
    --
    --   1. Spawn error (binary not found, permission denied, etc.)
    --   2. Non-zero exit status
    --   3. Stdout is not a valid JSON array of FeedItem
    --   4. Zero items emitted while stderr is non-empty
    --      (DegradedEmptyEmission below). Unlike buckets 1-3 this one is
    --      reached only AFTER a successful parse — the stdout was valid,
    --      it just carried nothing while the command complained.
    --   5. The epic's own configuration cannot be read, or the epic no
    --      longer exists / no longer carries a feed_command by the time the
    --      cycle claims it. These come BEFORE the exec: nothing is spawned.
    --      They exist because the cycle reads feed_command / feed_role /
    --      group_by_repo from the epic itself rather than accepting them
    --      from its caller (SerialisedFeedCycle), so an epic deleted or
    --      reconfigured between the request and the claim is a failure here
    --      rather than a sync against stale configuration.
    --   6. The epic is a role sub-epic carrying a feed_command at all — a
    --      provisioning bug (see RoleRoutedFeedSync). Reached after the
    --      parse, before the sync.
    --   7. The command exceeded feed_command_timeout (core.allium config)
    --      without exiting. It is killed and reaped rather than left to
    --      finish or orphaned; the exec never produces output for the
    --      degraded-emission guards to see, so this bucket is reached the
    --      same way buckets 1-2 are — before any parse. See
    --      SerialisedFeedCycle's bounded-cost note below.
    --
    -- Buckets 5 and 6 are unreachable in a correctly-provisioned, unraced
    -- system; they are specified because the cycle must do something
    -- definite when they happen, and "sync anyway" would be the wrong
    -- something.
    --
    -- The TUI is NOT notified of feed errors. Tasks already in the epic
    -- are unaffected.

    ensures:
        log_warn(epic.id, epic.title, error)
        epic.last_run = now()

    @guidance
        -- Implementation: src/feed/mod.rs::FeedRunner::tick spawns a
        -- feed cycle per eligible epic, and that job runs
        -- src/feed/cycle.rs::FeedCycle::run, which drives claim -> read config
        -- -> exec -> parse -> sync -> teardown. The command failure arms live
        -- at: spawn, timeout, and non-zero exit in
        -- src/feed/exec.rs::exec_feed_command, and the JSON parse in
        -- src/feed/parse.rs::parse_feed_items. Parse is shared
        -- exactly as exec is: the auto-poll path, the manual "r" refresh
        -- (src/runtime/epics.rs::exec_trigger_epic_feed) and the verify-feed
        -- CLI all call parse_feed_items, so the anti-drift argument
        -- FeedCommandStderrOnSuccess makes for the single shared exec holds
        -- here too — a change to parse semantics is made once (FeedItemParse).
        -- Bucket 4 is raised after the parse by the shared predicate in
        -- src/feed/exec.rs (see DegradedEmptyEmission).
        -- Bucket 5's two epic-read arms are pinned by the tests in
        -- src/feed/cycle.rs: the missing-epic arm by deleting the epic before
        -- the cycle runs, and the read-error arm by fault injection (renaming
        -- the epics table through a second connection to the same file). Both
        -- assert that the feed command never runs and that nothing is synced --
        -- the arms are otherwise unreachable, and an earlier version of the
        -- manual path defaulted the unreadable feed_role to none and synced
        -- FLAT onto a reviews_parent epic.
        -- Bucket 7 is exec_feed_command's tokio::time::timeout around the
        -- child (see SerialisedFeedCycle's BOUNDED COST note above for why
        -- src/process.rs::run_bounded couldn't be reused for this call site).
        -- Coverage: src/feed/exec.rs::exec_feed_command_returns_err_when_it_exceeds_the_deadline
        -- (the raw timeout, with a bounded elapsed-time assertion so the test
        -- doesn't merely wait out the hang) and
        -- src/feed/cycle.rs::a_hung_command_times_out_and_the_epic_recovers_on_the_next_cycle
        -- (the whole cycle: a hung command times out, fails, releases its
        -- claim, and a fresh cycle for the same epic afterward proceeds and
        -- syncs — proving recovery, not merely that one call returned).
        -- A future improvement is surfacing repeated failures in the TUI
        -- as an epic-level health badge, but that is out of scope here.
}

rule FeedCommandStderrOnSuccess {
    when: FeedCommandCompleted(epic, items)

    -- The warning is emitted by the exec layer BEFORE the stdout parse,
    -- so it also precedes a FeedCommandFailed outcome when the stdout is
    -- malformed. The stderr text is not carried on the event: it reaches
    -- callers on FeedOutput (see @guidance).

    -- A feed command that exits 0 while writing to stderr is NOT by
    -- itself a failure: its stdout parsed, and a NON-EMPTY emission is
    -- still synced. But it is the signature of a script that swallowed
    -- an internal error and emitted a degraded (often empty) array
    -- anyway, which FeedCommandFailure never sees. The stderr text is
    -- therefore logged at warn level so the evidence survives the run.
    --
    -- Stderr on a zero-exit run is NEVER merely diagnostic. It TAINTS the
    -- emission, and the two rules below say what a tainted emission is
    -- allowed to do:
    --
    --   - ZERO items + stderr  -> DegradedEmptyEmission. The command is
    --     treated as failed and NO sync runs at all.
    --   - items > 0 + stderr   -> DegradedNonEmptyEmission. The sync runs
    --     ADDITIVELY: what the emission CONTAINS is written, what it
    --     OMITS is not acted on. Nothing is removed, so nothing is torn
    --     down.
    --
    -- The single principle behind both: a tainted emission is evidence of
    -- what exists, never evidence of what does not. Absence from a tainted
    -- emission means nothing, because the reason a script soft-fails is
    -- precisely that it produces FEWER items than the truth.
    --
    -- This REVERSES a deliberate decision recorded here by #3900, which
    -- had seen this failure and chose to keep stderr diagnostic-only —
    -- the sentence it wrote said an empty emission still reconciles and
    -- still removes tasks absent from it. #3989 reversed that sentence
    -- for the empty case because the observed cost was unbounded data
    -- loss: a soft-failing script that exits 0 with an empty array wiped
    -- a whole reviews_parent subtree, worktrees and all. #4095 then
    -- extended the same reasoning to the non-empty case, which #3989 had
    -- left explicitly waved through as a recorded residual risk.
    --
    -- ACCEPTED COST of the reversal: a feed script that writes to stderr
    -- on EVERY run never removes anything. If it also legitimately emits
    -- an empty array on a day with genuinely zero items it is treated as
    -- failed INDEFINITELY; if it emits items it still syncs them, but
    -- additively, so genuinely merged or closed PRs never disappear from
    -- it. There is no escape hatch short of fixing the script. This is
    -- accepted because the script contract above already requires errors
    -- on stderr to come with a non-zero exit — a script that chatters on
    -- stderr while exiting 0 is already outside contract — and because
    -- the alternative is unbounded data loss. Note the asymmetry of the
    -- two costs: a stale card the user can archive by hand, against a
    -- force-removed worktree and a deleted branch that no one can get
    -- back.
    --
    -- The logged text is trimmed and truncated to 2000 characters: a
    -- verbose script must not be able to write unbounded output into
    -- app.log on every poll.

    requires: exit_status = 0 and stderr != ""

    ensures:
        log_warn(epic.id, epic.title, truncate(stderr, 2000))
        -- and then, when items.count > 0, the emission syncs ADDITIVELY
        -- (DegradedNonEmptyEmission). When items.count = 0 the sync is
        -- suppressed entirely by DegradedEmptyEmission instead.

    @guidance
        -- Implementation: src/feed/exec.rs::exec_feed_command logs the
        -- warning and returns the trimmed, truncated text on its
        -- FeedOutput so callers can surface it too. It is the SINGLE exec
        -- for both feed paths: they share src/feed/cycle.rs::FeedCycle::run,
        -- which is the single place it is called from, so neither can regress
        -- to a private spawn that drops stderr. The manual path surfaces it in the TUI as a FAILURE
        -- status line when the emission is empty (DegradedEmptyEmission
        -- via ManualFeedTrigger's failure arm); a non-empty emission with
        -- stderr surfaces the ordinary success line WITH the degraded
        -- suffix (DegradedNonEmptyEmission).
        -- The zero-items-vs-non-empty split is not re-decided per caller:
        -- it is TWO sibling predicates in src/feed/exec.rs —
        -- degraded_empty_emission and degraded_partial_emission, both
        -- (item_count, stderr) -> Option<reason> and mutually exclusive by
        -- their item_count arm — applied ONCE after the parse and before the
        -- sync in the shared src/feed/cycle.rs::FeedCycle::run, so the two
        -- feed paths cannot even in principle disagree about them. When BOTH
        -- return None the emission syncs and reconciles as normal, with the
        -- stderr logged and nothing else.
        -- Coverage: src/feed/exec.rs::exec_feed_command_captures_stderr_written_on_zero_exit,
        -- exec_feed_command_truncates_long_stderr,
        -- exec_feed_command_truncates_multi_byte_stderr_without_panicking and
        -- exec_feed_command_reports_stderr_of_exactly_the_cap_complete (the
        -- capture and the 2000-character cap);
        -- src/feed/mod.rs::tick_stderr_on_zero_exit_does_not_suppress_sync
        -- (one item alongside stderr still syncs — additively).
}

rule DegradedEmptyEmission {
    when: FeedCommandCompleted(epic, items)

    -- The guard. A feed command that exits 0, parses to ZERO items, and
    -- wrote to stderr is treated as a FAILED command, not as an emission
    -- meaning "everything is gone". This is the signature of a script
    -- that soft-failed internally and emitted an empty array anyway —
    -- exactly how #3900's incident ran: a wrong gh account made every
    -- repo-scoped query fail, each soft-failed to an empty array, and the
    -- reviews_parent subtree was wiped.
    --
    -- Effect: FeedCommandFailed (bucket 4). Logged at warn level,
    -- epic.last_run bumped, and NO SYNC RUNS AT ALL — not the upsert, not
    -- the reconcile, not the stale delete. Every task already in the epic
    -- or its subtree is left exactly as it was, including its worktree
    -- and tmux window.
    --
    -- The two neighbouring cases are unchanged, and are the boundary this
    -- rule must not cross:
    --
    --   - ZERO items with EMPTY stderr is a clean empty run. It still
    --     reconciles normally, and it still removes merged/closed PRs and
    --     every other task absent from the emission.
    --   - A NON-EMPTY emission still SYNCS regardless of stderr — one item
    --     alongside stderr is a sync, not a failure — but when the stderr
    --     is non-empty that sync is ADDITIVE, per
    --     DegradedNonEmptyEmission below. This rule owns only the
    --     zero-item arm; it never suppresses a non-empty emission.
    --
    -- The guard is applied AFTER the stdout parse and BEFORE the sync.
    -- Placing it after the parse is deliberate: malformed stdout with
    -- non-empty stderr already fails as a parse error, which is bucket 3
    -- of FeedCommandFailure.
    --
    -- The guard applies to EVERY feed epic uniformly — flat, grouped and
    -- reviews_parent alike — not only to the reviews_parent path where
    -- the incident was observed.
    --
    -- The cost of this guard is recorded on FeedCommandStderrOnSuccess
    -- above: a script that always chatters on stderr and legitimately
    -- emits nothing never reconciles again.
    --
    -- PARTIAL degradation is covered by DegradedNonEmptyEmission below,
    -- not by this rule. This rule fires only at items.count = 0; a
    -- partially degraded emission — e.g. some of scripts/fetch-reviews.sh's
    -- four repo-scoped queries soft-failing to [] while the others succeed
    -- — is non-empty and so falls to that rule instead. #3989 recorded that
    -- gap here as a residual risk and left it open; #4095 closed it.

    requires: exit_status = 0 and items.count = 0 and stderr != ""

    ensures:
        FeedCommandFailed(epic, truncate(stderr, 2000))
        -- and therefore, by FeedCommandFailure: log_warn and
        -- epic.last_run = now(). No FeedSync dispatch occurs, so no
        -- UpsertFeedTasks / GroupedFeedUpsert / FlatFeedReconcile /
        -- RoleRoutedFeedSync pass runs and no task is created, updated,
        -- moved or removed.

    @guidance
        -- Implementation: ONE shared predicate,
        -- src/feed/exec.rs::degraded_empty_emission, next to FeedOutput in
        -- src/feed/exec.rs, so the two feed paths cannot drift — the same
        -- rationale that put the role dispatch in the single shared
        -- src/feed/ingest/mod.rs::run_feed_sync_by_role. It is applied once,
        -- after parse and before sync, in src/feed/cycle.rs::FeedCycle::run,
        -- which logs it and returns a failed outcome. The two surfaces then
        -- present that outcome:
        --   - the auto-poll job adds nothing. last_run is already bumped by
        --     src/feed/mod.rs::FeedRunner tick before the job is spawned, so
        --     this lands exactly on FeedCommandFailure's existing contract.
        --   - src/runtime/epics.rs::exec_trigger_epic_feed fails with the
        --     predicate's reason, which CARRIES the stderr rather than being
        --     it: degraded_empty_emission returns "command emitted no items
        --     but wrote to stderr: <stderr>", so the status bar shows
        --     "Feed for '<title>' failed: command emitted no items but wrote
        --     to stderr: <stderr>" (ManualFeedTrigger). The ensures clause's
        --     truncate(stderr, 2000) names the stderr the reason embeds — the
        --     cap is applied once, by exec_feed_command, before the predicate
        --     ever sees the text.
        -- Consequence: the zero-item "command wrote to stderr" hint that
        -- #3900 added to ManualFeedTrigger is now UNREACHABLE — a
        -- zero-item run that wrote to stderr can no longer produce a
        -- success outcome at all — so that hint is deleted, superseded by
        -- this harder failure.
        -- Coverage: inline unit tests on the predicate in src/feed/exec.rs —
        -- degraded_when_zero_items_and_stderr_present (fires),
        -- not_degraded_when_zero_items_and_no_stderr and
        -- not_degraded_when_items_present_despite_stderr (both boundaries);
        -- src/runtime/tests/feeds.rs::exec_trigger_epic_feed_fails_on_degraded_empty_emission
        -- (the manual trigger reports a failure) and
        -- exec_trigger_epic_feed_degraded_empty_emission_does_not_delete_existing_tasks
        -- (the manual path ran no sync at all, not merely a quiet one);
        -- src/feed/mod.rs::tick_degraded_empty_emission_does_not_delete_existing_tasks
        -- (auto-poll leaves pre-existing subtree tasks untouched — the direct
        -- regression test for the reported wipe). The false-positive boundary
        -- is pinned by the existing tests that must stay green unmodified:
        -- src/runtime/tests/feeds.rs::exec_trigger_epic_feed_zero_items (a quiet
        -- empty run still reconciles) and
        -- src/feed/mod.rs::tick_stderr_on_zero_exit_does_not_suppress_sync
        -- (one item alongside stderr still syncs — additively, per
        -- DegradedNonEmptyEmission, but it is not suppressed).
}

rule DegradedNonEmptyEmission {
    when: FeedCommandCompleted(epic, items)

    -- The partial-degradation guard, and the answer to the question #3989
    -- left open on DegradedEmptyEmission.
    --
    -- A feed command that exits 0, parses to AT LEAST ONE item, and wrote to
    -- stderr is a PARTIALLY degraded emission: some of what it was asked to
    -- fetch came back, and some of it soft-failed. scripts/fetch-reviews.sh
    -- runs eight-plus scoped queries and each failure branch prints to stderr
    -- and substitutes []; a wrong gh account, an expired token or one
    -- rate-limited org therefore produces exactly this signature. It is the
    -- #3900 mechanism at partial scale, and it is MORE likely than total
    -- failure, not less — total failure needs every query to fail at once.
    --
    -- Effect: the sync runs, but ADDITIVELY. An additive sync performs every
    -- write that follows from what the emission CONTAINS — insert, field
    -- refresh, and (on the role-routed path) cross-role MOVE — and performs
    -- NO write that follows from what it OMITS. Concretely, every
    -- feed-as-source-of-truth removal clause in UpsertFeedTasks,
    -- GroupedFeedUpsert, FlatFeedReconcile and RoleRoutedFeedSync is SKIPPED
    -- for this cycle: no stale delete, no clearing of absent sub-epics, no
    -- parent sweep. Because nothing is removed, no TaskTeardown runs, and no
    -- worktree, branch or tmux window can be destroyed by a run whose own
    -- script reported an error.
    --
    -- WHY ADDITIVE RATHER THAN SUPPRESSED. DegradedEmptyEmission suppresses
    -- the whole sync because a zero-item emission has nothing to contribute.
    -- A partial emission does: the items it did return are real, and new PRs
    -- must still reach the board. Suppressing it would also make a feed whose
    -- script fails one sub-query on most polls permanently useless, whereas
    -- additive keeps it useful and merely conservative.
    --
    -- WHY NOT GATE THE TEARDOWN ON TASK STATE INSTEAD. The rejected
    -- alternative was to keep deleting, but refuse the TaskTeardown for a
    -- removed task that still carries live agent state (a worktree plus a
    -- non-terminal status), leaving the agent and its branch alive for the
    -- human to reclaim. Rejected because it pays its cost on the LEGITIMATE
    -- case: a PR merged while its review agent is still running is ordinary,
    -- and it is the exact case feed-driven teardown was added for. Under a
    -- state gate every such merge leaves a worktree, a branch and a tmux
    -- window on disk with no card left to find them from, forever. The
    -- failure this rule addresses is better fixed by not performing the
    -- wrong delete than by softening its consequences.
    --
    -- RESIDUAL RISK, stated honestly: this rule keys on stderr, so a feed
    -- script that degrades SILENTLY — soft-failing a sub-query without
    -- printing anything — is not detected, and its omissions still delete and
    -- tear down. The script contract (FeedCommandFailure) requires errors to
    -- be reported, so a silent soft-fail is a script bug; dispatch cannot
    -- distinguish it from a genuine removal. Unlike #3989's residual risk,
    -- this one has no known instance and no cheaper detector.
    --
    -- The rule applies to EVERY feed epic uniformly — flat, grouped and
    -- reviews_parent alike.

    requires: exit_status = 0 and items.count > 0 and stderr != ""

    ensures:
        -- The sync dispatches exactly as FeedSync says (by role, by
        -- group_by_repo), with removals disabled for this cycle.
        FeedSync(epic, items, additive: true)
        for task in Tasks where task.external_id != null and task.epic in epic.subtree:
            -- Nothing absent from a tainted emission is removed, whatever
            -- its status and whether or not it carries agent state.
            exists task

    @guidance
        -- Implementation: src/feed/exec.rs::degraded_partial_emission, the
        -- sibling of degraded_empty_emission and deliberately the same shape
        -- ((item_count, stderr) -> Option<reason>), applied once after the
        -- parse and before the sync in src/feed/cycle.rs::FeedCycle::run. The
        -- two predicates are mutually exclusive by their item_count arm, so
        -- the order they are applied in does not matter; the cycle applies
        -- the empty one first because it can return early.
        --
        -- The mode is src/feed/ingest/mod.rs::SyncMode {Reconcile, Additive},
        -- threaded from FeedCycle::run through run_feed_sync_by_role into
        -- every sync path. It is an enum rather than a bool so a call site
        -- cannot silently mean the opposite of what it reads.
        -- The mode is decided once, but its CONSEQUENCE is applied at five
        -- sites, in two shapes. Counting them honestly matters: a sixth removal
        -- path added later inherits neither shape automatically.
        --
        -- Shape 1 — pick the DB variant. The removal is inside the upsert, so
        -- there is no step to skip: src/db/queries/tasks.rs::upsert_feed_tasks_additive
        -- is the insert/update half of upsert_feed_tasks WITHOUT its per-epic
        -- stale delete, sharing one private helper with it so the two cannot
        -- drift in what they WRITE. It returns the same (empty) removed-row type
        -- as its sibling precisely so the three call sites that choose between
        -- them — src/feed/ingest/mod.rs::run_feed_sync (flat),
        -- src/feed/ingest/upsert.rs::upsert_role_groups and
        -- src/feed/ingest/grouped.rs::upsert_sub_epic_and_recalc — share one
        -- success/failure tail instead of restating it per mode.
        --
        -- Shape 2 — skip the call. The removal is its own phase, so the caller
        -- omits it: src/feed/ingest/role_routed.rs skips
        -- src/feed/ingest/stale.rs::delete_stale_subtree and
        -- clear_parent_stranded_tasks; src/feed/ingest/grouped.rs skips
        -- clear_absent_sub_epics and clear_parent_flat_tasks. A clearing pass
        -- takes no mode — clearing is a reconcile act by definition, so
        -- src/feed/ingest/grouped.rs::clear_sub_epic_and_recalc is mode-free
        -- and the decision to clear at all lives in its caller.
        --
        -- FeedSyncOutcome.removed is therefore empty on every additive path,
        -- so src/feed/mod.rs::cleanup_removed_feed_tasks is a no-op by
        -- construction rather than by a second guard.
        --
        -- KNOWN LIMIT of that choice: the destructive step has no defence of its
        -- own. Nothing at the teardown seam re-checks the mode — it cannot, the
        -- mode does not reach there — so a future path that leaks a row into
        -- `removed` on a degraded cycle destroys a worktree, which is this
        -- rule's whole subject. Empty-by-construction was preferred anyway
        -- because a second guard there would make the invariant ambiguous
        -- (which layer owns it?) while covering only the paths someone
        -- remembered to route through it. A new removal path's obligation is to
        -- take one of the two shapes above.
        --
        -- SURFACING. An additive cycle is not silent: a run that declines to
        -- remove anything must say so, or the user reads an unchanged board as
        -- a reconciled one. src/feed/cycle.rs::FeedCycleOutcome::Synced
        -- carries a `degraded: Option<String>` (the predicate's reason, which
        -- embeds the stderr) and the manual "r" refresh appends it to the
        -- success line — "Feed for '<title>': N task(s) synced (additive, no
        -- removals: <reason>)" — via FeedMessage::Refreshed. The auto-poll
        -- path logs at warn and adds nothing to the TUI, matching how it
        -- presents every other degraded outcome (FeedCommandFailure).
        --
        -- Coverage: inline unit tests on the predicate in src/feed/exec.rs —
        -- degraded_partial_when_items_present_and_stderr_present (fires),
        -- not_partially_degraded_when_zero_items and
        -- not_partially_degraded_when_no_stderr (both boundaries);
        -- src/db/tests/tasks.rs::upsert_feed_tasks_additive_upserts_without_deleting_absent_tasks;
        -- src/feed/ingest/tests.rs, one per removal mechanism:
        -- additive_role_routed_sync_keeps_a_task_absent_from_the_emission,
        -- additive_role_routed_sync_keeps_parent_stranded_tasks,
        -- additive_grouped_sync_keeps_tasks_in_a_dropped_repo_sub_epic and
        -- additive_flat_sync_keeps_a_task_absent_from_the_emission, plus
        -- additive_role_routed_sync_still_moves_and_inserts (additive is "no
        -- removals", not "no writes");
        -- src/tui/tests/epics.rs::feed_refreshed_degraded_says_it_removed_nothing
        -- (the status line names the withheld removals);
        -- src/feed/mod.rs::tick_partially_degraded_emission_does_not_delete_or_tear_down
        -- (auto-poll end to end, asserting the MockProcessRunner saw no
        -- `git worktree remove`) and the manual counterpart
        -- src/runtime/tests/feeds.rs::exec_trigger_epic_feed_partially_degraded_emission_does_not_delete.
        -- The boundary — a clean non-empty emission still reconciles and still
        -- tears down — is pinned by the pre-existing tests of UpsertFeedTasks
        -- and RoleRoutedFeedSync, which must stay green unmodified.
}

rule AppendOnlyFeed {
    when: FeedCommandCompleted(epic, items)

    -- An epic's standing declaration that its feed emits EVENTS, not STATE.
    --
    -- Every other feed epic MIRRORS an upstream: an open PR, an open CVE. The
    -- item is emitted for as long as the thing it describes is open, so its
    -- absence from a trusted emission is evidence that the thing closed, and
    -- the feed-as-source-of-truth removal in UpsertFeedTasks acts on that
    -- evidence.
    --
    -- Some sources have no "open" state to mirror. A log warning is the
    -- motivating one: it happened, once, at a point in time, and nothing
    -- upstream ever retracts it. Absence from a later emission is evidence of
    -- nothing at all — only that the scanned window moved on, or the file
    -- rotated. Read as a mirror, such a feed would tear down a task, and with
    -- it a worktree, a branch and a tmux window, on the very next poll after
    -- creating it.
    --
    -- core/Epic.feed_append_only = true is how an epic says which kind it is.
    -- The effect is exactly DegradedNonEmptyEmission's: the sync runs
    -- ADDITIVELY. Every write that follows from what the emission CONTAINS
    -- still happens — insert, field refresh, cross-role move — and every
    -- removal clause in UpsertFeedTasks, GroupedFeedUpsert, FlatFeedReconcile
    -- and RoleRoutedFeedSync is skipped. Because nothing is removed, no
    -- TaskTeardown runs.
    --
    -- WHY THE SAME MECHANISM AND NOT A NEW ONE. "Perform the insert-and-refresh
    -- half of a sync and none of the removal half" is behaviour the runtime
    -- already has; today it is reached only by accident, when a script
    -- soft-fails. An append-only epic wants it deliberately and permanently.
    -- A second mechanism with the same postcondition would be a second thing
    -- to keep correct, and the two would drift. There is exactly one additive
    -- path, and both causes reach it.
    --
    -- HOW SUCH A TASK IS EVER CLOSED. By the user, never by the feed. An
    -- ARCHIVED task keeps its row, its epic and its external_id (tasks.allium:
    -- ArchiveTask moves status and releases resources; it does not drop the
    -- row), so the ON CONFLICT arm of UpsertFeedTasks matches it on every
    -- later poll and the status-preservation clause leaves it archived. A
    -- re-emitted item whose task is archived therefore refreshes that archived
    -- task's fields and creates nothing new. Archiving is permanent
    -- suppression of an external_id, and it is the ONLY suppression the
    -- mechanism offers — dispatch keeps no separate record of ids it has
    -- retired. DELETING the task is NOT suppression: it removes the conflict
    -- target, so the next emission carrying that external_id inserts a fresh
    -- task. That asymmetry is a consequence of where the state lives, and it
    -- is the reason the user-facing instruction for these epics is "archive",
    -- not "delete".
    --
    -- ACCEPTED CONSEQUENCE: every later poll re-applies the item's title,
    -- description, tag, labels and sort_order to that archived task and bumps
    -- updated_at — forever, for as long as the source keeps emitting the id.
    -- Only status and sub_status are preserved.
    -- This is ordinary UpsertFeedTasks behaviour rather than anything new, but
    -- an append-only epic is where it becomes permanent and visible, because
    -- archived rows there accumulate instead of being deleted as stale. It is
    -- accepted, not overlooked: the refresh is what keeps an OPEN card's
    -- occurrence count current, the same clause cannot tell an archived row
    -- from a running one, and column ordering is by sort_order rather than
    -- updated_at (core/Task), so the churn reorders nothing a user sees.
    --
    -- INTERACTION WITH THE DEGRADED-EMISSION RULES. The causes of additivity
    -- are independent and neither overrides the other; additive is their
    -- union. An append-only epic whose command also writes to stderr is
    -- additive for both reasons, with the same single effect.
    -- DegradedEmptyEmission still suppresses the whole sync, and this rule's
    -- guard defers to it: being append-only is not a reason to trust a
    -- zero-item emission from a command that reported an error. The guard
    -- likewise defers to FeedCommandFailure on a non-zero exit. This rule
    -- fires only where a sync would have run anyway, and changes only whether
    -- that sync removes.
    --
    -- THE FLAG IS NOT RETROACTIVE, IN EITHER DIRECTION. Turning it ON stops
    -- future removals; it does not restore a task an earlier trusted cycle
    -- already removed. Turning it OFF makes the very next cycle a mirroring
    -- one, which removes every accumulated task the current emission does not
    -- name — for a log feed, potentially all of them. That is the honest
    -- consequence of the flag meaning what it says, and it is why the flag
    -- belongs to wiring an epic up rather than to a board keybinding. The epic
    -- header does still SURFACE the flag, read-only and with no key hint
    -- precisely because there is no key (epics.allium: AppendOnlyEpicIndicator).

    -- NOT COMBINABLE WITH GROUPING OR A FEED ROLE. An epic may be append-only
    -- OR grouped/role-routed, never both, and the write path REFUSES the pair
    -- (epics.allium: UpdateEpicViaMcp). This was first imposed as a
    -- conservative guard against a migration deadlock, pending a decision on
    -- whether the combination was wanted at all. THAT DECISION IS NOW MADE and
    -- the answer is no: the restriction is permanent, and the reason is the
    -- grouping KEY, not the sync machinery.
    --
    -- REPO IS A MIRRORING FEED'S KEY. Every mirroring feed's item IS a
    -- repo-scoped thing — a PR, a CVE, a Dependabot alert each belongs to
    -- exactly one repo and carries that repo's URL as its own. group_by_repo
    -- partitions such an emission along an axis its items already have.
    --
    -- AN EVENT FEED'S ITEMS ARE NOT KEYED BY REPO. They are keyed by where in
    -- the code they fired. A log record's identity is its level, its emitting
    -- module and its static message head; the record carries no URL at all.
    -- The url such an item does carry is a single configured repo root, set
    -- for one unrelated purpose: so dispatch can resolve a local clone and the
    -- card can be dispatched into a worktree. Grouping by repo would therefore
    -- partition EVERY item into one sub-epic named after that repo — or, with
    -- no repo configured, into one named "other" (repo_name(url)'s fallback).
    -- Grouping by a constant: a level of nesting and no information.
    --
    -- AND A MULTI-REPO EVENT FEED DOES NOT RESCUE IT. Given one epic scanning
    -- several repos' logs, repo is the least useful of the axes those items
    -- carry. Level and occurrence count are already a label and sort_order;
    -- the module is the locus a fix lands in, and it is what such a board
    -- wants grouped. group_by_repo is the only grouping key the runtime has.
    -- So the want that would justify combining the two is not "group my log
    -- feed by repo" but "group it by module" — a different feature, and one
    -- nothing has asked for.
    --
    -- WHAT TO DO INSTEAD, at no cost in code: ONE FLAT APPEND-ONLY EPIC PER
    -- REPO, all under a common parent. FeedTick schedules off a flat epic
    -- list, so a feed epic nested under a parent is polled exactly like a root
    -- one. That yields the same board shape and the same status rollup as
    -- grouping would, and adds a per-repo cadence and a per-repo off switch
    -- that a single grouped epic could not offer. This is the supported route,
    -- and the refusal message points at it.
    --
    -- THE MIGRATION DEADLOCK WAS A SYMPTOM, NOT THE REASON. For the record,
    -- because the earlier framing of it was wrong and should not be revived:
    -- the grouped and role-routed paths gate their MIGRATION steps on the same
    -- permission as their stale deletes, so an epic that is additive by
    -- configuration would defer that migration forever. The former framing
    -- called re-homing an accumulated task an undefined operation. It is not
    -- undefined — FlatFeedReconcile's re-home and RoleRoutedFeedSync's move
    -- both define it, identically, as a MOVE of the existing row. Only
    -- GroupedFeedUpsert migrates by copying into the sub-epic and then
    -- clearing the parent wholesale, and that is a defect of ITS OWN, harming
    -- ordinary MIRRORING epics: the copy is a fresh row, so the migration
    -- discards the original's status and hands its worktree and tmux window to
    -- TaskTeardown. Fixing that is worth doing on its own merits and does NOT
    -- reopen this question — the key argument above stands without it.
    -- Consequently there is no reason to split SyncMode's single permission in
    -- two ("may I delete because absent" versus "do I trust this emission
    -- enough to migrate"); that proposal is retired.

    requires: epic.feed_append_only and exit_status = 0
              and (items.count > 0 or stderr = "")

    ensures:
        -- The sync dispatches exactly as FeedSync says (by role, by
        -- group_by_repo), with removals disabled — permanently, for this epic.
        FeedSync(epic, items, additive: true)
        for task in Tasks where task.external_id != null and task.epic in epic.subtree:
            -- Absence from an append-only feed's emission is not evidence of
            -- anything, so nothing is removed, whatever its status and whether
            -- or not it carries agent state.
            exists task

    @guidance
        -- Implementation: the flag is read alongside the two degraded
        -- predicates in src/feed/cycle.rs::FeedCycle::run, which selects
        -- src/feed/ingest/mod.rs::SyncMode::Additive when EITHER a degraded
        -- predicate fires OR the epic is append-only. Everything below that
        -- point — run_feed_sync, sync_grouped_feed,
        -- run_role_routed_feed_sync, upsert_feed_tasks_additive — is
        -- unchanged and untouched by this rule: it already implements "no
        -- removals" and does not care why it was asked for.
        --
        -- SURFACING. An append-only cycle is a normal, healthy cycle, so it
        -- is NOT reported the way a degraded one is. DegradedNonEmptyEmission
        -- appends "(additive, no removals: <reason>)" to the manual-refresh
        -- status line because withholding removals there is a symptom; here
        -- it is the configured behaviour, and reporting it every poll would
        -- train the user to ignore the line that matters. A cycle that is
        -- additive for BOTH reasons still reports the degradation.
        --
        -- Coverage: src/feed/cycle.rs for the three mode-selection cases this
        -- rule adds — append-only alone, append-only alongside a degraded
        -- emission, and the clean mirroring boundary that must keep removing.
        -- Degraded-alone is NOT here; it belongs to DegradedNonEmptyEmission
        -- and is covered where that rule's own guidance says it is.
        -- src/feed/ingest/tests.rs covers the reconcile behaviour, reusing the
        -- existing additive_* assertions rather than duplicating them, and
        -- src/db/tests/tasks.rs covers the archive claim above
        -- (upsert_feed_tasks_leaves_an_archived_task_archived_and_inserts_no_second_row,
        -- which pins the delete asymmetry in the same test).
}


-- FeedItemParse (conceptual): the JSON decode of a feed command's stdout into
-- Vec<FeedItem> lives in ONE shared function, src/feed/parse.rs::parse_feed_items,
-- called by ALL THREE feed entry points:
--
--   - src/feed/cycle.rs::FeedCycle::run        (BOTH runtime paths: the
--     auto-poll job and the manual "r" refresh share this one cycle)
--   - src/main.rs::cmd_verify_feed             (verify-feed CLI)
--
-- One function rather than three literal serde_json calls, because N call sites
-- are N places a change to the feed wire format can land in one path and not the
-- others -- the drift shape that produced the reviews_parent parent-flat bug in
-- the sync layer.
--
-- Two properties of that centralisation are load-bearing:
--
--   - The DECODE RULES are NOT per-caller and never were. Strict `tag`/`url_type`
--     rejection and lenient `signals` dropping belong to FeedItem's Deserialize
--     impl (src/models/tasks.rs::deserialize_lenient_signals for the lenient
--     half), so every caller gets them identically and none can opt out.
--   - AReviewTaggedFeedItemNamesItsPr is the one CROSS-FIELD rule, and it is the
--     one thing parse_feed_items validates itself. An item whose `tag` satisfies
--     core/TaskTag.is_review must carry a `url` that types as pr; anything else
--     — an empty url, or one that types as issue, security_alert or other —
--     REJECTS THE WHOLE EMISSION, exactly like an unknown `tag`, and for the
--     same reason: both are producer bugs a silent drop would hide.
--
--     Why not in Deserialize with the others: serde's field attributes cannot
--     see a sibling field, so a cross-field rule there means a shadow struct
--     duplicating all ten fields — the parity-drift shape this repo already
--     guards against elsewhere. parse_feed_items is the single decode point for
--     all three entry points, so a check placed there is just as uniform for
--     everything that arrives over the wire.
--
--     WHAT IT DOES NOT COVER, deliberately: the rule guards the DECODE, not the
--     STORAGE boundary. upsert_feed_tasks does not re-validate, so a FeedItem
--     built as a struct literal reaches the DB unchecked. No production path
--     does that — the only two decoders are FeedCycle::run and cmd_verify_feed,
--     both via parse_feed_items, and nothing outside tests constructs the
--     struct — but several DB-layer tests do, and they are asserting ingest
--     rather than decode. Adding a second enforcement point there would put a
--     mid-transaction failure on a path with no unguarded producer, so the
--     boundary stays where the wire is. A test fixture that could not have come
--     off the wire is still worth fixing rather than exempting, which is what
--     the ones affected by this rule got.
--
--     WHY THE RULE EXISTS. Both shipped producers already satisfy it by
--     construction: each emits `url` straight from `gh`, which is always a
--     /pull/ URL, and core/UrlType.infer types anything containing /pull/ as
--     pr. So the rule costs nothing today and pins what the dependabot runbook
--     is entitled to assume — that a FEED-created review task names a PR the
--     feed's own author filter already passed. See
--     AReviewRunbookCarriesOnlyTheBranchThatApplies in dispatch-prompt.allium, whose
--     author-check omission is gated on that provenance.
--
--     BLAST RADIUS, ACCEPTED. fetch-reviews.sh emits ONE array for the whole
--     review board, so a single non-conforming item empties the board for that
--     cycle rather than costing one card. That is the same trade the strict
--     `tag` rule already makes, and it is preferred here for the same reason: a
--     missing card is the failure mode that goes unnoticed.
--   - PRESENTATION of a parse failure is deliberately NOT shared: the manual path
--     renders a status-bar string, verify-feed prints the message plus a
--     500-character preview of the offending stdout. Only the decode is common.
--
-- Note the CALLER SETS differ between the shared layers: the parse is shared by
-- all three entry points, the exec (exec_feed_command) by only the two runtime
-- ones -- verify-feed deliberately spawns its own `sh -c`, having no
-- epic_id/epic_title and wanting terminal output rather than app.log (see
-- FeedCommandStderrOnSuccess). run_feed_sync_by_role is the single role
-- dispatcher (see the FeedSync block below).
--
-- Implementation: src/feed/parse.rs::parse_feed_items, re-exported from
-- src/feed/mod.rs.

-- FeedSync (conceptual): single dispatch point for feed upsert. Every dispatch
-- carries the cycle's MODE alongside the emission: reconcile (the default —
-- write what the emission contains, remove what it omits) or additive (write
-- what it contains, remove nothing). The mode is decided once per cycle by
-- DegradedNonEmptyEmission and is orthogonal to the routing below: all three
-- paths honour it, and none of them re-decides it.
-- Routing precedence by core/Epic.feed_role:
--   1. feed_role = reviews_parent -> RoleRoutedFeedSync (subtree reconcile).
--   2. otherwise epic.group_by_repo = true -> GroupedFeedUpsert.
--   3. otherwise -> FlatFeedReconcile: first re-home any tasks stranded in the
--      epic's active RepoGroup sub-epics back to the parent and delete the
--      now-empty sub-epics, THEN UpsertFeedTasks on the parent. The re-home
--      step is the OFF-side counterpart to GroupedFeedUpsert's ON-side
--      migration; it heals a feed epic whose grouping was just toggled off
--      (epics.allium ToggleGroupByRepo), which flips the flag but moves no task
--      synchronously. When the epic has no RepoGroup sub-epics this reduces to
--      a plain UpsertFeedTasks, exactly as before.
-- The reviews_parent path lives in its own entry point (run_role_routed_feed_sync),
-- separate from run_feed_sync, because it orchestrates a whole subtree rather
-- than an epic's own emission. The WHOLE feed_role dispatch — including the
-- reviews_parent -> run_role_routed_feed_sync branch, not just the
-- flat/group_by_repo branches — lives in ONE shared dispatcher function,
-- src/feed/ingest/mod.rs::run_feed_sync_by_role, which BOTH FeedRunner.tick
-- (auto-poll) and exec_trigger_epic_feed (manual "r") delegate to. Because the
-- switch is centralised in that single function rather than duplicated per
-- caller, the two paths are structurally identical — not merely behaviourally
-- equivalent by convention — for every feed_role. A manual refresh therefore
-- cannot fall back to a flat upsert for an epic whose feed_role selects a
-- different path; doing so would strand feed tasks in the wrong epic (the
-- reviews_parent parent-flat bug).
--
-- Implementation: src/feed/ingest/mod.rs::run_feed_sync_by_role is the shared
-- role->path dispatcher; it delegates to src/feed/ingest/mod.rs::run_feed_sync (flat/grouped) or
-- src/feed/ingest/role_routed.rs::run_role_routed_feed_sync (reviews_parent).
-- Both runtime paths reach run_feed_sync_by_role through the single shared
-- src/feed/cycle.rs::FeedCycle::run, which reads feed_role from the epic
-- itself; neither surface performs its own feed_role switch, and neither can
-- pass a stale one down.

-- == Manual trigger ==

rule ManualFeedTrigger {
    when: ManualFeedRefresh(epic)

    -- A feed epic is any Epic where feed_command != null. The "r" key is
    -- only active when such an epic is selected (card highlighted on the
    -- kanban board, or focused inside the Epic view). Pressing "r" on a
    -- non-feed epic has no effect.
    --
    -- The command runs immediately — it does not wait for the next
    -- FeedTick interval. The FeedRunner's last_run timestamp for this
    -- epic is NOT updated by the manual trigger; the next automatic poll
    -- fires at its normal scheduled time.
    --
    -- Status bar feedback (contrast with FeedCommandFailure, which is
    -- silent):
    --   - On start:   "Fetching feed for '<title>'…"
    --   - On success: "Feed for '<title>': N task(s) synced"
    --   - On ADDITIVE success: the same line with a suffix naming why nothing
    --     was removed — "Feed for '<title>': N task(s) synced (additive, no
    --     removals: <reason>)". See DegradedNonEmptyEmission. A cycle that
    --     declined to reconcile must not present as one that reconciled and
    --     found nothing to do.
    --   - On failure: "Feed for '<title>' failed: <error>"
    --   - When DROPPED: "Feed for '<title>' is already refreshing…"
    --
    -- The dropped line is the third outcome, and it is NEITHER a success nor a
    -- failure: a cycle for this epic was already in flight, so per
    -- SerialisedFeedCycle this request ran nothing at all. It is surfaced rather
    -- than swallowed because the alternative is a keypress that looks like a
    -- no-op. Note the honest limit of the reassurance it gives: it means "a
    -- cycle is running", not "fresh data is arriving imminently" — if that
    -- in-flight cycle is stuck on a hung command, every subsequent "r" keeps
    -- reporting this for up to feed_command_timeout (core.allium config)
    -- before the feed recovers on its own (SerialisedFeedCycle's BOUNDED
    -- COST).
    --
    -- There is no zero-item stderr hint on the success line. #3900 had
    -- one (" — command wrote to stderr (see app.log)", gated on N = 0 AND
    -- the command having written to stderr while exiting 0); that exact
    -- combination is now DegradedEmptyEmission, which produces the
    -- FAILURE line instead, so the hint is unreachable and gone. The
    -- additive suffix above is not that hint returning: it fires on the
    -- OPPOSITE arm (N > 0 with stderr), and it reports a decision dispatch
    -- took — removals were withheld — rather than merely pointing at the log.

    requires: epic.feed_command != null

    ensures:
        status_bar = "Fetching feed for '{epic.title}'…"
        -- Spawns epic.feed_command; on success:
        --   FeedSync(epic, items)
        --   status_bar = "Feed for '{epic.title}': {count} task(s) synced"
        -- on an ADDITIVE success (DegradedNonEmptyEmission — count > 0 AND
        -- the command wrote to stderr while exiting 0):
        --   status_bar = "Feed for '{epic.title}': {count} task(s) synced
        --                 (additive, no removals: {reason})"
        -- on failure (including DegradedEmptyEmission — count = 0 AND the
        -- command wrote to stderr while exiting 0, whose {error} is the
        -- stderr text):
        --   status_bar = "Feed for '{epic.title}' failed: {error}"
        -- and when SerialisedFeedCycle drops the request, no command is
        -- spawned at all:
        --   status_bar = "Feed for '{epic.title}' is already refreshing…"

    @guidance
        -- Implementation: src/runtime/epics.rs::exec_trigger_epic_feed, which is
        -- presentation only — it builds a src/feed/cycle.rs::FeedCycle, awaits
        -- it, and maps the outcome onto the four status lines above. The
        -- dropped arm is a distinct FeedMessage variant (AlreadyRefreshing),
        -- not a Failed with a special string, so the status bar cannot blame the
        -- user's feed command for a serialisation decision.
        -- Keybinding: src/tui/input/normal.rs::handle_key_normal, KeyCode::Char('r').
        -- The keypress gate ("is this a feed epic at all?") is still answered
        -- from the TUI's loaded board in
        -- src/tui/update/feeds.rs::App::handle_trigger_epic_feed — that is this
        -- rule's requires clause and cached data is fine for it. But the values
        -- the cycle ACTS on (feed_command, feed_role, group_by_repo) are read
        -- fresh from the epic inside FeedCycle::run, never passed down from that
        -- snapshot, so a manual refresh cannot run a command or a grouping mode
        -- the user has since changed.
        -- FeedSync dispatches by epic.feed_role IDENTICALLY to the auto-poll
        -- path (see the FeedSync dispatch block above): feed_role =
        -- reviews_parent -> run_role_routed_feed_sync; otherwise
        -- group_by_repo=true -> sync_grouped_feed / false -> upsert_feed_tasks,
        -- both via run_feed_sync. exec_trigger_epic_feed must therefore read the
        -- epic's feed_role (not just group_by_repo) and branch on it; a manual
        -- "r" refresh on a reviews_parent epic MUST route into the My/Team/Bots
        -- subtree, never flat-upsert into the parent. Coverage:
        -- src/runtime/tests/feeds.rs (manual trigger on a reviews_parent epic routes
        -- like auto-poll and strands no feed task on the parent);
        -- src/runtime/tests/feeds.rs::exec_trigger_epic_feed_fails_on_degraded_empty_emission
        -- and exec_trigger_epic_feed_degraded_empty_emission_does_not_delete_existing_tasks
        -- (the failure line, and that no sync ran behind it);
        -- src/runtime/tests/feeds.rs::exec_trigger_epic_feed_removed_task_tears_down_its_worktree
        -- (the manual path also hands its FeedSyncOutcome.removed to
        -- src/feed/mod.rs::cleanup_removed_feed_tasks).
        -- FeedCommandFailure (auto-poll failures) remains silent — only
        -- manual trigger failures surface in the status bar.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- OneFeedCycleAtATimePerEpic (prose invariant): at most one feed cycle per epic
-- is in flight at any moment. Enforced by SerialisedFeedCycle. Stated as prose
-- rather than as an expression-bearing invariant because a cycle is an
-- in-process activity, not entity state — there is nothing to quantify over.
--
-- This is what makes RoleRoutedFeedSync's phase ordering sound. Its steps are
-- separate non-transactional writes, each filtering on a task's CURRENT epic,
-- and the argument that "the move lands before the deletes, so a moved task is
-- never reported as removed" holds WITHIN one cycle only. Two cycles for the
-- same epic interleaving across those writes breaks it, and because the
-- stale-delete feeds TaskTeardown, breaking it destroys a live agent's worktree
-- rather than merely losing a row.
--
-- Deliberately scoped to "another CYCLE", not "another writer": a concurrent
-- non-feed writer moving a task into the reconciled subtree can still produce
-- the same outcome. See SerialisedFeedCycle's scope paragraph.

-- A reviews_parent epic is a pure router: it holds NO feed-managed task
-- directly. Every feed task (external_id != null) in its subtree lives in one
-- of the role sub-epics (my_reviews / team_reviews / bots) or their children,
-- never flat on the parent. RoleRoutedFeedSync maintains this by moving any
-- parent-stranded feed task down to its routed role sub-epic and by deleting
-- stale parent-stranded feed tasks. A violation means an emission bypassed the
-- role router with a flat upsert (the manual-trigger / stale-parent-task bug),
-- which also deadlocks the subtree-uniqueness constraint
-- (core/Epic enforce_feed_task_subtree_unique_insert) on the next reconcile.
-- Manual (external_id = null) tasks on the parent are permitted and unaffected.
invariant NoFlatFeedTasksOnReviewsParent {
    for epic in core/Epics where feed_role = reviews_parent:
        not epic.subtasks.any(t => t.external_id != null)
}

-- The floor binds the RESOLVED cadence, not merely the stored one, so the
-- fallback an unset epic inherits must itself clear the floor. Were
-- default_feed_interval to sink below min_feed_interval, leaving the field
-- blank would poll faster than the fastest value a user is permitted to
-- enter, and the floor would be advice rather than a bound. The two are equal
-- today; raising the default is safe, lowering it past the floor is not.
invariant DefaultFeedIntervalClearsTheFloor {
    core/config.default_feed_interval >= core/config.min_feed_interval
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Notifications
------------------------------------------------------------

-- After a successful upsert (regardless of whether any tasks actually
-- changed), the runner sends one McpEvent::EpicChanged(epic_id) on the
-- notification channel so the TUI reloads only the affected epic and
-- its tasks — not the whole board. Failed runs do NOT send a refresh.

-- FlatFeedReconcile re-homing and subtree uniqueness (resolved): the
-- feed-task subtree-uniqueness triggers (enforce_feed_task_subtree_unique_
-- insert/update, migration v72) key their "role sub-epic" branch on
-- feed_role NOT IN ('none', 'reviews-parent') — this matches ANY
-- command-carrying feed_role, including a plain feed epic like CVE
-- (feed_role = cve), not only reviews_parent. So the triggers DO cover a
-- plain feed epic's own parent+RepoGroup subtree.
--
-- This is safe by construction, not merely by convention: the Feed Task
-- Identity Invariant guarantees at most one task row per external_id within
-- the subtree at any time. FlatFeedReconcile's re-home step therefore MUST
-- move the existing row (UPDATE epic_id, the same mechanism
-- src/service/grouping.rs::flatten_epic already uses via set_task_epic_id),
-- never delete-and-recreate — a move relocates the sole existing row for
-- that external_id and can never collide with itself, whereas a
-- delete+recreate would (even if only transiently) risk two rows sharing an
-- external_id in the subtree.

-- Insert-trigger self-conflict on in-place updates (resolved, migration
-- v76): the "SAME role -> fields updated in place" path above (== Global
-- identity and the move ==) resolves via `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT(epic_id,
-- external_id) DO UPDATE` (UpsertFeedTasks' statement, reused here). SQLite
-- fires BEFORE INSERT triggers before resolving the ON CONFLICT arm, so the
-- v72 enforce_feed_task_subtree_unique_insert trigger — whose EXISTS check
-- had no exclusion for the row ON CONFLICT was about to update, unlike the
-- sibling update trigger's `t.id != OLD.id` — matched the task's own prior
-- row and aborted, on EVERY re-upsert of an already-tracked task. Because
-- UpsertFeedTasks batches a whole epic's item list in one transaction, this
-- silently dropped any other new task batched alongside the false-positive,
-- not merely the offending one. Migration v76 adds `t.epic_id !=
-- NEW.epic_id` to the trigger's EXISTS check: safe because the
-- tasks_epic_external_id unique index (v38) already guarantees at most one
-- row per (epic_id, external_id), so the excluded match can only ever be the
-- self-row ON CONFLICT resolves against — never a second, distinct row, and
-- never a genuine cross-epic duplicate elsewhere in the subtree.
